editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:*Fifth-day. Ninth sitting. Forty minutes by the exchange's clock. The house's counsel on the remedy: that no seat has ever before ordered a practitioner into a frame over a provision that stands. Jent: that the code allows it, and the petition asks for nothing the code doesn't give. Both true. The hood wrote for some time and said nothing. We came home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:9:*Seventh-day. The lodge, with Seln. The road column: none, none, the post, a carter with slates for the stable roof. Four coats. He says the bundle under his table is as thick now as his hand is wide, and that he'll write another on the next Seventh-day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:13:*Fifth-day. Tenth sitting. Eleven minutes. The evaluation seat renewed its request to the Archmarshal's office for a certificate on the protocol, and the hood minuted the renewal. That was the whole of the morning. Nobody at our table looked at anybody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:*That's a decision too. I'm entering it as one. Not deciding, every day, on purpose, by all five of us, with a date on it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:27:He had looked at *five* for a while after he wrote it, and then he had let it stand. Seln had counted five beans on a gardener's table and called it arithmetic, and Cael did not think there was a better word for it yet.
