editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:61:The eleventh sitting took nineteen minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:65:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The eleventh sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:93:"The merits are concluded. The seat sets the matter down for closing." The unhurried voice went on, with no more weight on these words than on any others it had spoken that year. "Two closing sittings, in the last month of the academic year, a week apart. The ruling will be given upon the last Fifth-day of that month. The parties will be notified of the hours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:101:Five weeks to the last Fifth-day. Two sittings, and then a hood reading from a single leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:107:"The last Fifth-day," he said. "I should like it remembered that I asked somebody to write that down in the year's first week, at a refectory table, and that I had a reason." He looked in at Brom, who had ridden down on the box that morning and was riding back inside, in the corner, with his coat buttoned. "The district has had a standard panel listed for that day since the fourth. At the second bell, three streets from here, in a hall I am told is draughty. The seat will not have looked to see what else is sitting in the town. Seats do not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:127:It was a plain room, like all the rooms on that corridor, with a bed and a press and a table under the window, and it was tidier than Cael's because Brom was. The two boxes were under the bed, roped, where they had been since the week of the gate, and Cael had stopped seeing them. The plaque, dark wood and a brass plate with the bar and ring, lay on the shelf on its face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:"Four weeks," he said. "To the day. I worked it out on the box, coming down, last Fifth-day, after the hood said it." He did not look round. "I thought I'd like to stop carrying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:145:He had never asked. None of them had. It had been agreed in the recess before the year opened, in about as many words as it takes to pass the salt, that what came to Brom from Velmere was Brom's, and that nobody would ask what it offered or what he meant to answer. He had seen Brom's face, once, on the evening the first of this year's letters was opened, and had gone on not asking. He had watched that hand go to that pocket a dozen times a day through a road and a cache and a recess. He did not know one word of what was in either envelope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:"I know you've none of you asked," said Brom. He might have heard him think it. "All year. I noticed. I heard you agree it, in the recess, Lira and Karis and you. I was coming back along the walk with the bread and I stopped to let you finish." He turned round at last and sat down on the edge of the bed, heavily, with his forearms on his knees. "It's the kindest thing anybody's done for me on this hill. I'd like four more weeks of it, if that's all right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:165:The year's last month opened on a First-day, and Bracken turned his card.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:173:*Closing sitting: the second Fifth-day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:175:*Closing sitting: the third Fifth-day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:177:*Ruling: the last Fifth-day. The house will attend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:207:*Deposited for safe keeping, the first day of the year's last month. Three boxes, roped. Depositor: K. Dellenmoor. Contents: the depositor's own papers. To be released to the depositor, or to her order.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:213:It lasted perhaps as long as it takes to read a line. Bracken had written *or to her order* in his own hand. He had lent an old enrollment book for a citation and had not asked what for. He had read every leaf the house had filed all year, and counted the sittings, and seen the last Fifth-day coming since the year's first week. He was a registrar of thirteen years' standing, and there was nothing whatever in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:237:He thought about them for a while, the four men down there, senior, patient, changed on the bell. They had stood at that road since before the year opened, and they had been waiting all that time for one thing, a change in a column. A status. The riders had come, and the road, and after that nothing; and they had gone on standing there through a recess and eleven sittings with their tin cup, because the thing they were waiting for was being brought to them, a Fifth-day at a time, by the people they were watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:None of them had said they were leaving. He did not think any of them had decided it, in so many words, even alone. It was only that there were now three boxes on a shelf, and two under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat, and a new strap on a satchel, and five beans in a gardener's jar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:251:*First-day. The year's last month.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:255:*The seat: two closing sittings and a ruling, the last on the month's last Fifth-day. Leave for Karis to be heard is "to be considered at the close." Brom's panel sits the same day, at the second bell, three streets off.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:257:*On Brom's table: one envelope, sealed, square to the edge. Four weeks now, less a few days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:9:"Sixth sitting," said Bracken, to nobody. "I said eleven. I should like it noted that I now say fourteen, and that I said so in the second month, and was thought gloomy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:19:The young officer at the side door had got over his cold at last, and he had the log open on its board, and the first name already written, in the hand of a man who had written it so often that he no longer saw it. Cael read it upside down as he went past, as he had read it at every sitting since the fortieth day. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* And in the second column, under *standing*: *the enrollee of record, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:Nothing in it had changed. He had half thought something would, after the recess, after Ostrand and the borrowed office four streets back, as he had half thought the hill would look different on the morning after the case. But the wool exchange smelled of lanolin and damp coats, as it always had. The forty benches ran back to the doors, a quarter full. Lot seventeen was cut into the floor under his left boot. On the platform the hood sat in its place behind the long table, grey and still, and below it the five chairs of the evaluation seat stood in their row with their covers off and five people in them. Four were district evaluators whose names he had heard read once and had written down; and in the middle, with a file open on her knee at its usual angle, sat Ilsev.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:47:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The sixth sitting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:"The respondent filed, in the week before the recess, a supplemental table of the renewals of this enrollment, with the day of each sitting and the day of each seal set side by side. I have read it. I read it twice, on a boat." He turned his head a little toward the house's table, toward Karis, and inclined it. "It is correct. The petitioner accepts it as filed, in every line, and adopts it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:67:"And lastly," said Jent. "A great deal was said at the preliminary about the evaluator of record at Halcenvane. I said some of it. I should like it in this minute, in plain words, that the petitioner accepts that officer as competent in every particular, accepts his panels as properly held, and accepts every figure he has signed as honestly come by." He let his hands fall. "The petition relies upon none of these matters. It never has. I mention them only so that nobody is put to the trouble of proving what I do not dispute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:89:"The seat notes the petitioner's acceptance of the evaluator of record. It will nonetheless have his notes before it. The respondent's house is ordered to produce that officer's notes upon the enrollee for the current academic year, entire, at the eighth sitting. So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:It was. A production order was the most ordinary paper a seat could make. Bracken would carry it up the hill tonight in his coat, and Gault would read it in his office behind wing three with his instrument case very straight on the bench beside him, and would say that he had written it down. There was nothing in Gault's notes that could hurt anybody. There was a year of figures in them that breathed and never settled and never went past a red line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:153:The fourteenth line, on both leaves, was a designation with its title written out in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:163:He did not look across the room. He had looked at that boy before, on the pump step and at Greyvane and on the sprung oak at the bluff, and he remembered every one of the looks, and he would not give himself another. He had decided that long ago, and the deciding had held through eleven weeks of Fifth-days in the same long room, forty feet from the house's table. It held this morning. He went down both leaves to the foot and found them alike in everything but the one line. Then he wrote *superseded* in the corner of the first, in the plain hand he kept for corners, with *sixth sitting* under it, and entered the second in the receipt book against the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:175:The merits were heavier than the first sittings had been. There was not more talk; there was less. But every sitting put paper on his table, and all of it had to be receipted and numbered and laid against the lists. At the sixth sitting, the respondent's supplemental upon the renewals: eleven leaves, a table, a certified copy of a minute. At the seventh, a week later, the petitioner's acceptance of the tournament office's record, in one leaf; the respondent's second supplemental, upon the manner in which that record had been kept, in nineteen; and a correction slip, signed by the registrar of the house, altering the date on one exhibit by a single day, with the reason given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:179:Havel numbered it and pasted it in beside the line it corrected, and squared it with his thumbnail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:181:The house's exhibits stood at a hundred and seventy-nine by the end of the seventh sitting. He had receipted every one, and in eleven weeks he had not found among them a leaf out of order, a seal smudged past reading, or a count that did not come to what its contents leaf said it came to. He had sat junior to a good many proceedings. He had never sat junior to a record like it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:185:At the inn by the bridge, on the night of the seventh sitting, he ate his supper in the long room downstairs among the carters, because the fire was better there, and then went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:He had thought, this morning a week ago, with the dry pen resting under a fourteenth line, that there might be a seventh. He could admit that here. For the space of perhaps a breath it had seemed to him that he was looking at something: the first sheet's mark, written out at last in a clerk's fair copy, in a schedule, on his own blotter. For four years he had known it only as a mark. Now it had been given a line and a number in a paper before a seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:209:He thought instead, for a while, of the words on his rotation order. *The seat's Fifth-days, until relieved.* He had read them in the first week as he read any order, for what they required of him. He read them differently tonight, in his head, with the river going by. A man could be relieved because the work was done. He could also ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:He had never asked to be moved off anything in his life. He did not ask now. He only noticed, as he might have noticed a door in a wall he had walked past for years, that the order had the word in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:233:"Not expel. Nobody has written that word. *Release.* *Come to an accommodation.* One of them wants me to *spare the house a ruling*." Her voice was quite even. "Their reasoning is not foolish. If this seat rules that those sections can be turned upon a person for what he is, that ruling stands in the registry's books for every house on the continent. If I let you go before it rules, there is no ruling. One boy leaves one hill, and thirty-four charters sleep sound. The man who put it most plainly has been my friend for twenty years. He wrote it in his own hand, and it cost him something to write."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:The slips were the house's ordinary form for the recess, half a sheet, with a printed line for the enrollee's name and a printed line under it that said *Remaining in residence. Reason:*. Lira had written *the program*, and Brom *the frames*, and Karis had written *research standing, per council*, in her small upright hand, with the date of the council's sitting after it, because she could not leave a reason uncited. Cael had stood at the counter with the pen for a while and then written *the proceedings*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:23:That was all. Nobody had asked the four of them whether they would go home for the recess, and nobody had needed to. The fifth was staff, and had no slip to sign.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:45:*Seventh-day. Down by five ways, up by five. A man read something in a borrowed room and thanked the room, not us, and went home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:49:*The recess starts tomorrow. Nobody goes home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:65:"Rooke says the program doesn't stop for the recess," she said. "Rooke says the registry doesn't stop for the recess, so why should he." She pushed the honey toward him. "There are six of us. Me, the Current girl, the Force, the Stone, Rooke and the clerk. The clerk counts as one of us because she says so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:89:"The hall keeps no recess," said Bracken, before he could ask. "Enrollees go home. Paper does not." He turned the daybook round on the counter so that Cael could read the morning's entry, which was a thing he did only when he was pleased with it. *Recess, the first day. In residence: forty-one enrolled, by slip. Post: the small bag. Nothing sealed.* "Nothing sealed," he repeated, and turned it back. "I have written that at the foot of the day for a week running, and I find I look forward to it. Go and be idle somewhere. It is what a recess is for, and I am told it does not come naturally to everybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:97:Hesk's letter came up on the Third-day, in the recess bag, which was a quarter the size of the term bag and which Seln sorted at the post-room trestle in eleven minutes by the clock over the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:215:On the Seventh-day he walked the lodge with Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:217:They had done it every Seventh-day since the inventory, and the recess did not change it, for the lodge-man let the wing read the arrivals book on a Seventh-day, as he had let it for longer than anybody remembered, and Seln read it as he read everything, from the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:"They have not doubled either." He turned back a page. "In the term before the cut, I could read them weekly by the grade of the man. A junior on the market day, a senior on the sitting day. Since the cut, they are all senior, every bell, and they do nothing." He put his finger under *None*. "Nobody at the gate. Nothing in the post that should not be in it. No new face in town at the inn on the wharf. Not a letter, not a knock, not a rider."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:*Seventh-day of the recess's first week. The road column: none, none, the post, none. Four coats, senior, every bell. No third move. Seln's reading of the silence, unchanged: why spend a team when the seat will deliver him at the year's close.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:245:When he had blotted it he went to the window. Across the dark court the wing's counter was shut, and behind it one lamp was burning at the copying table, as it burned every Seventh-day night of the year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:249:"This week's will say that the subject remained in residence for the house's recess, with three associates, on slips lodged at the counter. That he went down to the town on the Seventh-day before it, as the hill does. That he was not observed on any floor, there being no floor in a recess. Nothing further to report." He had walked a few steps. "Every word of it true. I will write another next week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Lira crossed the east hall again on the First-day of the recess's second week, and this time she did not know she was doing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:55:"The trade cannot," said Seln. "Not here, and not by me." He set his own spoon down. "The road can. It is not a secret. Carters know it, and drovers, and anybody who has slept out in the rain twice and decided to be drier the third time." He considered. "Tomorrow, at the second bell after noon, in the lower orchard, if the weather holds. In coats."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"The lower orchard," said Karis. "You can see the lower orchard from the bluff road's second switchback."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:63:"With a good one." Seln took up his spoon again. "A thing done in the open goes into a log as what it is. Five people in an orchard on a recess afternoon, learning to pitch a camp from the wing's assistant, who is known to have travelled. If anybody writes that down, he will write it down in those words, and then he will turn the page. A thing done in a cellar goes into a log as whatever the man with the pencil is afraid of." He ate. "I would always rather be written down as what I am doing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:65:So they went down to the lower orchard on the Second-day, in coats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The house's farm lay on the back of the bluff, on the long south slope away from the river, where the wind was less. Below the stable passage were the kitchen gardens in their walls, and below the gardens the upper orchard and the lower, old trees in rough grass with their branches bare and black with wet. Below the lower orchard a stream came down out of a fold in the slope and ran along the bottom hedge toward the water meadows, loud over stones. The farm track went down beside it, and at the orchard's foot, where the track met the stream, stood a small stone cottage with smoke going up from it, which was the gardener's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:101:The open approach took the rest of the afternoon, and the gardener enjoyed it more than anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:Lira went first, quickly and lightly, with her chin up, and Cael, watching from the top, saw her eyes go to the cottage's door, and the gap beside the woodpile, and the gate into the meadow, one after the other, before she was halfway there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:The gardener opened his window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:Karis went second, at a steady pace, with her hands held a little out from her sides as if she were carrying a tray, and stopped at the right distance. She would have done well, the gardener said, if she had not then stood and read the date cut into his lintel, with her lips moving, for as long as it took him to wonder what she wanted with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:125:"He's counting my windows," said the gardener.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:He came down the track at the slow heavy walk he used for everything, in the very middle of it, with his big hands hanging open at his sides. He was not looking at the cottage; he was looking at the gardener, plainly, as he would have looked at a man across a counter. He stopped where the others had stopped, and stood there and waited, with the grey sky behind him, and did not shift his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:135:The gardener looked at him for quite a long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:137:"That one," he said at last, "I'd ask in." He sounded almost disappointed. "He looks like he'd mend the gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:143:They sat in the gardener's kitchen for the last hour of the light, six of them round a table made for two, and Seln did the watches there, on the scrubbed boards, with five dried beans out of the old man's jar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:He looked at the five beans for a moment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:157:Nobody said anything else about it. Karis wrote *two of four* in her notebook and drew a ring round it, and the gardener poured the tea, and told Brom at length what was wrong with the top hinge, and Brom listened to all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:161:"Oilcloth," said Seln. "And then a second oilcloth, because the first has a seam. Pencil and not ink; ink runs, and a wet pencil line only goes soft. Nothing written goes at the bottom of a load, where the water gathers." He turned his cup a quarter turn on the gardener's scrubbed boards. "And whatever you would grieve to lose rides on a person and not on a cart. A cart can be left behind in a hurry. People generally bring themselves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:181:They went out by the orchard gate, and Seln wrote it in the lodge book himself before they went. *Five, to the farm, by the orchard gate. To return before the first bell.* The lodge-man read it over his spectacles and said that in his day people had gone to the farm at night for other reasons, and went back to his fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:183:There was no moon, and the cloud had not lifted in four days. Past the stable passage the dark came down like a sack, and Cael stood in it at the top of the farm track with the others breathing round him and could not see his own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:185:"Four hundred yards," said Seln's voice, to his left. "The track goes down beside the orchard wall to the meadow gate. I am going ahead. Somewhere between here and the gate I shall be sitting, and I shall count what I hear. You are to walk it so that I count nothing." A pause. "You will halt three times. I leave it to you where. At every halt, somebody is to make sure of the party. Decide who, now, before you move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:201:He had not known how loud he was. On a floor he was quiet; he had been quiet on floors for years. On a farm track in the pitch dark, with ruts under his boots that he could not see, he was a cart, and every stone turned. Karis in front of him was worse, for she had something in her coat that creaked, a notebook's board against a pencil tin, and at every step it said so. Only Lira made no sound at all; and Brom, behind him, was so quiet that twice he thought the hand on his shoulder belonged to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:203:The new thing woke up at the first gateway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:207:*Gateposts*, he told it, without moving his lips. *The orchard's. It's a gap in a wall. Leave it be.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:It did not ask, for the quiet thing from the stair never asked. It only offered, as it had offered every day of his life for two years, and it offered now with a kind of patience, as if it had been waiting since supper for him to see the sense of it. A man was sitting somewhere ahead in the dark, counting, and Cael was trying not to be counted; and here in him, sealed and paid for every morning since he woke, was the one thing he owned that could take him down that track past any man alive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:225:They walked. The second halt came at the bottom of the orchard wall, where the stream's noise began to come up through the dark, and Brom went along the line again, a hand on each head. "Four. On."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:"I counted the lady with the notebook eleven times," he said. "I am sorry. You will have to carry your pencils in something that does not talk." Karis took the tin out of her coat and looked at it as if it had betrayed her. "The boy, I heard on every stone from the gate to the first halt, and after that only twice. You learn fast, and your boots are wrong." He turned to Lira. "You I did not hear at all. You I saw, once, against the sky at the orchard corner, because you stood up straight to look ahead. On a road, do your looking from lower down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:259:"I walked a pace and a half behind him for three hundred yards," said Seln, "and I never once heard him put a foot down. I have known men who were paid for that." He shielded the candle with his hand. "It is the first time in a good many years that anybody has come back up a road in the dark to look for me. I had forgotten what it was like." He said it to the candle, in his counter voice. Then he blew the candle out. "Again, from the gate. Up the hill this time, which is harder. And you count me too. I am on the end of the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:295:She nodded. Then she took a breath, and let it out, and said, in a much lighter voice, that the gardener's dog had more sense than the gardener, and that she had half a mind to go down after supper and tell them both so. She talked about the dog the whole way to the refectory, and he let her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:"Program trial," said Rooke. "My rule. You both know it, and I'll say it anyway, because it goes in the book." He did not get up. "Four exchanges. A flag to whoever takes each one, or to nobody. Most flags has it. Level after four, the table rates you both. Three bursts for the Wind; past three the card pays. The Current, what her Path allows." He nodded at the clerk. "Rated. In this book. Go when the Stone drops his hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:31:The landing beat was late. It was not late by much, only by the length of a step, but the whole of Lira's fighting was built on that beat, the instant after a burst when the floor took her weight and gave it back and she could go again. She had it on the left foot, half a pace past where she had meant to be, facing a little the wrong way. The place where the hip was going to be was behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:105:She came out past him into the covered walk with her wraps half off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:179:*Sixth-day, recess. Lost to the Current, two flags to one. Landed left every time and didn't know it. Cael saw on the First-day. Rooke saw. I didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:She read it over. It was all true, and it did not make her feel any better, and she left it exactly as it was. That was the rule of the book. If she was going to carry it past the last station, there was no use in its being kind to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:233:There was a floor sheet for him at the wing's counter, the first in fourteen days, and the desk clerk pushed it across without looking up. *Fourth-day, wing three. Supervised, per provision.* Under it, in the same hand, a line he had not seen on a sheet since before the recess: *Fifth-day: the seat. Excused all floors.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:237:*First-day. The term after the recess. Four hundred back on the hill by noon. The coats counted them in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:241:*Sixth sitting on the Fifth-day. Wing three the day before. The rotation's written. I know what both of them will say before I walk into either room.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:He would not let the house's escort have them, or Bracken, or the coach's boot. He rode inside, on the eighth sitting's morning, with the bound volume on his knees in the house's boards and both hands laid on it, and the case with the handle set very straight on the seat beside him, as if the instruments in it might be wanted to swear to something. He was a spare, exact man with a magister's grey at his temples, and Cael had sat across a trestle from him every week for two years and had never before seen him out of a room he was in charge of. He looked at the wet streets of Ostrand going by with a kind of mild disapproval, as a man looks at a column somebody else has added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward," said the seat's clerk. "Upon the merits. The eighth sitting. Production, under the seat's order of the sixth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:"Entire." Gault's hand was still on the board. "I was asked for the notes upon the enrollee. I have taken that to mean every note in which he appears, and not only the notes of his bouts. There are seventeen panel notes, one to a supervised session, with the three cards and the figure. There are also five notes that are not of any panel." He lifted the hand. "I mention it so that the seat is not surprised by the leaf for the year's seventy-sixth day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:39:The seventy-sixth day. Four riders at the gate, and an order read aloud under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:45:"*Seventy-sixth day. An order was presented at the house's gate, by four riders, in the name of an office styled facilities, for the enrollee's attendance upon an assessment. No assessment of this enrollee has been notified to the evaluator of record, by any office, at any time. I have written that down.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:51:"The petitioner has no knowledge of the matter in that note," said Jent. "I say so for the minute, and I say it carefully. It is no part of the petition. No instrument of the kind described was sought by the petitioner or issued at his instance." He paused. "As to the rest of the volume: the petitioner accepts it, as he said he would. He relies on none of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:83:It was some days after the eighth sitting, on an afternoon of hard bright cold, and he had come up the four flights with a question about the ninth that he forgot at the door. The reading room's table stood bare to its cloth, with the chairs pushed in, and the lamp in the window was not lit, because it was day. And under the window, on the boards, with her back against the wall and her knees up and a square of planed deal across them, sat Karis, writing in pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:173:On the Fourth-day of the tenth sitting's week he fought in wing three for the last time that year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:259:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. The evaluation seat constituted for these proceedings, having first given notice in the year's eighth week that certification would be required, now renews its request to the Archmarshal's office for certification of the standard protocol, as applied to a re-evaluation of the enrollee of record at Halcenvane, should such be ordered. The seat's closing sittings are set down for the year's last month. The office's certificate is requested before the first of them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:265:The first notice had come in the year's eighth week and he had acknowledged it by return, in one line, as he acknowledged every notice. He remembered writing the line, and he had meant, then, to sign the certificate when it was asked for. The protocol was sound. He had helped to write two of its sections as a young man, and had defended the whole of it before three convenings, and in all his service he had never refused his name to a thing he knew to be sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:279:*The Archmarshal declines to certify.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:19:"Then I shall leave you to it." Bracken turned to go, and turned back, as he always did. "Under load," he said. "I asked the district clerk what load. He wrote back that it is the assessors' to set on the day, and that in his experience they set it at what the title was won against, and a little over." He nodded once to each of them, the small nod, and went out into the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:39:It always began at the wrists. It was not a colour, exactly, though the lamp found a dull grey in it, like a blade that has been oiled and put away. It was more that the surface of him stopped behaving like skin, and stopped creasing. It went up his forearms and across his shoulders under the shirt and down into his back, and where it had gone, Brom looked less like a man holding still and more like something that had been built there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:89:They fought it on the Crown yard's sprung oak in the afternoon, under a white sky, with the house on the tiers. Rooke had chalked it on the yard's board himself. *Exhibition. Wind, Iron One, Halcenvane. Blade, Iron Five, the lake house. Five exchanges at most. Two touches ends it.* Three cards at the table: the lake house's instructor, the Mire instructor from the wing, and the Stone, who sat between them with his big hands folded and looked as if he had been carved there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:113:"Touch," said the steward. "Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:129:He stood, with the lath hanging in his right hand and its tip a few inches off the floor, and let her run the whole count at him, three lies and the truth, and did not move for any of it. He was reading. A Blade's line ran out past the end of his edge, and where it ran he knew what would cross it, a breath before it crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:Cael saw it and for a breath his stomach turned over, because it looked like the old thing, the touch and the step. It was not the old thing. Her right foot touched the oak and left it again, on purpose, lightly, like a hand taken off a hot kettle, and she was over the lath and down on the left, a full pace past him, with the beat under her and her back to his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:161:"Touch," said the steward. "Halcenvane. Two touches to one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:Rooke had put it at the top, above the week's drills, and ruled a chalk line under it, so that nobody could take it for anything that would be wiped off on the Seventh-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:197:"You'd better." Rooke turned from the rain at last, and looked at her, and then for a moment past her at Cael, with a face that gave away exactly as much as his board did. "Twenty-eight is a figure. It isn't the figure. Wraps. Every count ends on whichever foot I call, and I'll call it late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:He priced it at his sill, by the lamp, in four lines. *Night, the east hall, L. alone. Everything public, at the rate, to win. Buys: she knows. Spends: hip past five on boards; shoulder; ten counts for a lane, twelve for a third; behind the eyes. Not the quiet thing. Not the new one.* He looked at *she knows* for a moment and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:Fifteen feet of it, from his chalk to the space behind her left shoulder, a third deep. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the cracks of the boards woke and ran, pale, down the lane, and under the lanterns it threw a thin moving shadow, and he saw her see the shadow and know it. She had learned to read that band on this floor in the year's seventh week. She burst along its edge to get out from under where he would arrive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:259:It carried him a third faster than any feet. Her burst had been along the lane's edge; he came down the lane's middle and out of its end, past her, behind her, while her landing beat was still coming up through her knees, and touched her between the shoulder blades, where she had touched the Blade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:285:He let her strike, and she put three fast palms into his raised forearm, one, two, three, and the give took all three like a cup filling, and the fourth she did not throw, because she had counted that as well. He closed the distance on the third and touched her wrist, on the tape, with one bright contact of Karis's spark. She yelped and shook her hand, and by then his other palm was on her collarbone. He went past five on the boards and felt the hip begin to write its bill, crest to knee, and went on, a sixth and a seventh, and the push again, and the read never once off her.

