   157	It was not the foot. She had known that since Rooke turned his board round. It was the asking. Every time she came down, there was a moment, shorter than a blink, when she asked the foot whether it would hold, and waited for its answer, and the waiting was the step. The only cure for asking was to land so often that the answer got dull. Rooke had said a hundred more. She thought it would be nearer a thousand, and that she would do them, here, at night, where the Current girl did not have to watch her and be sorry.
   158	
   159	She took the book out of her coat.
   160	
   161	It was not much of a book. She had bought it from the chandler by the bridge on the day she carried the program's chalk, for less than the price of a meal: a thing the size of her hand, stitched, with an oilcloth cover, of the kind carters kept their loads in. She kept a stub of pencil pushed down its spine. There was nobody's name on it anywhere.
   162	
   163	She had started it in the dairy week. Not that night; the night after, when she could not sleep for thinking about three carters and a road with four days of stamps on it and then none. She had thought then, lying on her back in the dark, about what she actually owned. Not the program. The program was Rooke's, and the east hall was the house's, and the red book belonged to a river academy and was only lent. The figure on the steward's sheet was hers, but it was hers the way a room is yours when somebody else holds the key; it meant something because a hall's seal was on it, and it would go on meaning something exactly as far as that seal was honoured.
   164	
   165	Four days east, loaded, it would be a piece of paper with a number on it.
   166	
   167	So she had started writing down what she knew. Not what she had won. What she knew, in her own words, in pencil, in a carter's book, so that it would still be hers out where no hall's seal went with it.
   168	
   169	She turned the pages under the lantern. *FLOORS*, on the first leaf, in the capitals Karis hated. Sprung oak: six, and it gives you back more than you put in, so go early. Boards: five, and they're honest. Slate: four, and it takes the beat out of your knees by the third. New planks laid crossways: they slide, and nobody tells you. Chalk road in wind: four, and mind the ditch. She had underlined *ditch* twice and then, a week later, drawn a small rude face beside it.
   170	
   171	*PEOPLE*, after that. A page for the Force: he builds on four, or on two when he's hurrying; you can see it in his knees. A page for the Silver at the confluence, with *go on your fifth* at the top in a box. A page for the Stone, which had almost nothing on it, because he never said anything, only a list of the places she had found him standing. Half a page for the Current girl. *Watch the front hip. Go along the bend.*
   172	
   173	She took the pencil out of the spine and wrote under it.
   174	
   175	*She learns in the bout. Third exchange, she broke the glide into pieces. Fourth, she stood where I'd land.*
   176	
   177	And then, on the leaf at the back that she had headed *ME* and had not much liked writing on:
   178	
   179	*Sixth-day, recess. Lost to the Current, two flags to one. Landed left every time and didn't know it. Cael saw on the First-day. Rooke saw. I didn't.*
   180	
   181	*It isn't the foot. It's asking the foot.*
   182	
   183	*Forty tonight. Four in five by the end. Tomorrow, right.*
   184	
   185	She read it over. It was all true, and it did not make her feel any better, and she left it exactly as it was. That was the rule of the book. If she was going to carry it past the last station, there was no use in its being kind to her.
   186	
   187	---
   188	
   189	She did not get up at once.
   190	
   191	The lantern hissed, and up in the dark of the gallery something small moved along the rail and was still. She sat with the book on her knee and her bare right foot flat on the cold boards, on purpose, and let herself think about the sheet.
   192	
   193	Four losses. Rooke would send her program sheet down to the district seat with the meet record, when the time came, and a clerk at a counter would lay the two side by side. On one, a figure of twenty-seven against a Silver of eleven years' standing. On the other, *Not fought. Not fought. Not fought.* and a red flag from a girl three ranks up who was her friend. The clerk would not know about a ditch. There was no column on the sheet for ditches.
   194	
   195	She found that she minded. That surprised her a little. She had thought, after the reading room, after the forty pairs and the father's letter that she had read aloud in her own voice, that she would never again be able to care what a clerk at a counter laid beside what. She knew what the counter was. She knew what sat behind every certificate in the wing's drawer, her own among them, three lines in a hand she had never seen.
   196	
   197	She had wondered about that, once. Only once, on the second night, on the stair. What had the keeper read on her, before anybody wrote it down? She remembered a brass plate, and cold fingers turning her by the shoulder, and being told to stand still. She did not remember anybody saying a word she could use.
   198	
   199	She had put it away on the same stair. There was no finding out. And she had spent four years being told what she was by people who had never watched her fight, and she was not going to start telling herself the same kind of story now, in the other direction, with no more to go on than they had.
   200	
   201	Wind, first rank. That was what the paper said. What she was, she was writing down herself, in pencil, in a carter's book.
   202	
   203	And still she wanted the ladder. She could say that, here, where nobody would write it. Rooke had rebuilt a hall for her. He had brought a man from the river to stand on crosses, and bought a red book, and put her name on a slate in his own hand, and nobody had ever done that for her in her life. At Fenmark they had told her that her form was correct and her room was wrong, and shown her the yard out the back. For a year after that she had stood at guild doors. This was the first door anybody had built to her measure and held open.
   204	
   205	She knew now what it opened into. Karis had said it: both doors, the same room. If she climbed every rung Rooke had set for her and stood before the advancement sitting and was rated Silver, she would be a Silver in a registry that struck children's margins as a matter of form. She would be on the paper, and the paper was the thing in the case.
   217	She did not say before what, and he did not ask.
   218	
   219	"It won't be pretty," he said.
   220	
   221	"I didn't ask for pretty."
   222	
   223	"I'll price it first. That's the rule, and it's initialled."
   224	
   225	"Price it, then. But don't score it." She unfolded herself from the coping. "Tenth bell. Bring the book if you must. I'm not writing on my hand."
   226	
   227	He priced it at his sill, by the lamp, in four lines. *Night, the east hall, L. alone. Everything public, at the rate, to win. Buys: she knows. Spends: hip past five on boards; shoulder; ten counts for a lane, twelve for a third; behind the eyes. Not the quiet thing. Not the new one.* He looked at *she knows* for a moment and left it.
   228	
   229	The hall was cold, and the shutters were down, and she had lit both lanterns and chalked two marks on the painted floor thirty feet apart. She was at the south one in her wraps with her weight even, and he left his coat and the satchel on the bench and went to the north.
   230	
   231	"No table," said Lira. "No count. We go till I say stop. If I touch you, I'll say so. You needn't."
   232	
   233	"All right."
   234	
   235	"Don't be kind."
   236	
   237	He stood on his chalk and let go of the room he had been fighting inside all year.
   238	
   239	That was what it felt like. He had lived inside the house's rate since Norhold as a man lives inside a coat, four on boards and never a fifth, two contacts, the give shut, the push a flat hand, the lane nowhere. He did not take the coat off; he let it fall, and stood there in the lantern light with all of it awake at once, and it was so much easier than holding it that his eyes stung.
   240	
   241	She ran the count.
   242	
   243	He did not wait for the fourth. The read had her weight before her front foot had finished its lie, deep, to the hips, and it said where the truth would come from a full beat before she spent it. He went first. Her own burst, the first thing he had ever taken, low and flat off the left foot, straight across the count; and he saw her eyes widen, because nobody had ever simply walked through it before. She burst to meet him, late. He was already down, and the landing beat gave him back, and he put the push into the boards at her landing place a hand's breadth before her foot arrived.
   244	
   245	It was not a flat hand but the push at fighting load, the full shove of the thing, and the air over that square yard of floor went solid for an instant and then gave. Her foot came down on nothing where she had expected, and she sat down hard, with his palm already on her shoulder.
   246	
   247	Three seconds, perhaps. He stepped back.
   248	
   249	Lira sat on the painted floor with her mouth open.
   250	
   251	"Again," she said.
   252	
   253	She did not run the count the second time. She stayed back, out of the push's reach, moving on her toes along the far line of crosses, making him come to her, which was exactly right. He did not come; he asked the air to lean.
   254	
   255	Fifteen feet of it, from his chalk to the space behind her left shoulder, a third deep. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the cracks of the boards woke and ran, pale, down the lane, and under the lanterns it threw a thin moving shadow, and he saw her see the shadow and know it. She had learned to read that band on this floor in the year's seventh week. She burst along its edge to get out from under where he would arrive.
   256	
   257	He took the read off the lane and gave it to her, the way he had learned to, and stepped in.
   258	
   259	It carried him a third faster than any feet. Her burst had been along the lane's edge; he came down the lane's middle and out of its end, past her, behind her, while her landing beat was still coming up through her knees, and touched her between the shoulder blades, where she had touched the Blade.
   260	
   261	Then the bill came in.
   262	
   263	The warm hand over the inside of his skull. Everything slow. He stood where he was and began the count, as he always did. *One. Two.*
   264	
   265	She had turned. She was looking at his face.
   266	
   267	*Three.*
   268	
   269	"That's the ten," she said softly, and went.
   270	
   271	She came straight at him, burst on burst, her second and her third with no count between them, and he called the Wind to go and it came a beat behind his asking, and he called the read and it came late, and he got his forearm up and opened the give and it opened *late*, and by then her palm was flat against his ribs, on the right side, exactly where they had been cracked.
   272	
   273	"Touch," said Lira. "Mine."
   274	
   275	*Eight. Nine.* He stood with her hand on his ribs and waited for ten, and at eleven the hand lifted off the inside of his head.
   276	
   277	He was grinning; he could feel it, and he could not remember when he had last grinned on a floor.
   278	
   279	"You went inside the count."
   280	
   281	"I've written that count on my palm since the first week of the year. Did you think I'd never spend it?" She was grinning too, breathing hard. "Again. And don't lay a lane, or I'll do it twice."
   282	
   283	After that he did not give her anything.
   284	
   285	He let her strike, and she put three fast palms into his raised forearm, one, two, three, and the give took all three like a cup filling, and the fourth she did not throw, because she had counted that as well. He closed the distance on the third and touched her wrist, on the tape, with one bright contact of Karis's spark. She yelped and shook her hand, and by then his other palm was on her collarbone. He went past five on the boards and felt the hip begin to write its bill, crest to knee, and went on, a sixth and a seventh, and the push again, and the read never once off her.
   286	
   287	She was very good, better than she had been yesterday in the Crown yard, and twice she found a line he had not shut and was a hand away from him.
   288	
   289	It did not matter. He was not faster than her. He could see her understand that, somewhere in the eighth exchange or the ninth. He was only earlier. Everything she did, he had an answer for a half beat before she did it, and the answer came from a different drawer every time.
   290	
   291	Once, when she planted both feet to change direction, the new thing lifted its head at them. *There?* He named them without breaking stride. *Lira's. Leave them.* It cost him the half beat, and she nearly had him a second time.
   292	
   293	"Stop," said Lira.
   294	
   295	He stopped.
   296	
   297	She was bent over with her hands on her knees, and he found that he was not standing very well himself. His right hip had stopped writing and begun to shout, and his shoulder had counted the pushes and had a view about the last one, and there was a slow thick ache behind his eyes that was the lane and the naming together.
   298	
   299	She straightened up, and looked at him for a long moment in the lantern light. She was not frightened, as he had been afraid, on the wall, that she would be. Her face was the one she had turned on the Blade's lath, held out to her across his palms: somebody had offered her a thing, and she was deciding whether it was hers to take.
   300	
   301	"Right," she said. "Now I know."
   302	
   303	"What do you know?"
   304	
   305	"What it's going to look like." She sat down on the boards, all at once, with her legs out. "When somebody finally gets that in daylight, with a table and a yard full of people. I know what their face is going to do." She wiped her forehead with her wrist. "And I know you liked it."
   306	
   307	He did not answer at once.
   308	
   309	"Yes," he said.
   310	
   311	"Good. You should. You've been walking round this hill for two years like a man carrying a full pail up a stair." She looked at the chalk mark he had left, thirty feet away. "I'm not going to tell anybody about tonight."
   312	
   313	"Not Brom?"
   314	
   315	"Not Brom. He'd want to know if you were all right, and you are, and I'd have to say it six times. Not Karis; she'd want it measured, and it isn't a figure. Not the captain, ever, though he'd give his chart to have stood where I'm sitting." She shook her head. "It isn't going on my hand and it isn't going in my book. Somebody ought to have seen it once who wasn't keeping score. That's all it was for. It's mine."
   316	
   317	"The costs go in the Log. That's the rule."
   318	
   319	"The costs. Hip, shoulder, eyes. Not the exchanges." She held out a hand, and he pulled her up, and his hip told him what it thought of that. "Not that I had one off you."
   320	
   321	"You had one off me."
   322	
   323	"I know I did," said Lira, with great satisfaction. "I just don't want it written down."
   324	
   325	He wrote the bill at the bench, standing, because sitting had become a question. *Hip, crest to knee, from the sixth. Shoulder, three pushes at load. One lane: ten. A naming in the middle of it. Behind the eyes till morning, I'd guess.* He stopped with the pen lifted. Then he added, because the book was to be kept honest and this was a cost too: *It was easier than the band, by a long way. Mind that.*
   326	
   327	She was at the great door when he had done, with her hand on its edge.
   328	
   329	"*Door*," she said. "*It's held.*" And then, as he went through it: "You were early. Every time. I'm going to think about early for a month."

