editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:63:"He asked to your face, in front of people. He didn't go to the provost and he didn't start a rumor; he walked up and asked." She shrugged. "Half the people at Fenmark would have smiled at me in the corridor and written a letter about me the same afternoon. Give me a boy who asks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:165:She came off her mark before he had finished setting his feet, not at the textbook distance but inside it, fast, the way she had fought on the Ardenmere circuit when the rent was due, in a style no academy had ever taught her and Fenmark's records had never once managed to describe. The Force Path threw a push to drive her back out, and it was a good push, a real one, the air in front of his palm going thick and hard as a wall. She could have slipped it. Cael saw the space where she could have slipped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-08.md:195:"The file says Copper. Fenmark's file. It's in a box at the intake desk, and it says Copper, and it says I was trouble, and it says the termination stands." She set the towel over her good shoulder. "The floor just watched something else. I'm not asking the file to change. I wanted the floor to have seen it. Now it has."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:123:"At Fenmark," she said, "a man came round every morning with a little brass level and a cloth, and straightened every frame on every wall in the wing before the first bell. Every morning. I used to watch him do it." She took her hand away. "Nobody here's going to come round with a level."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:177:The read showed it to him as something he had no picture for. The weight of the floor did not stop at the captain's guard. It thickened. It went on into the guard and grew slower and heavier and harder to see, layer under layer under layer, as the light grows heavier going down into deep water, until somewhere a long way in it was too heavy to read at all. It lay in front of the captain in a broad arc, chest-high, from his left hand round to his right and a little way behind him on both sides, and it did not stand still. All of it moved, all the time, very slightly, the way water moves when nothing is moving it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:Fenmark: the house that had Kindled her at fourteen and read her Copper and sent her out into the world with six letters on a certificate. It had a fighter in the region's Iron bracket this year, a second-year, and Rooke had given him four words: *Good. Schooled. Expects respect.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:115:"I know it happened. I read it. I added it up twice in my head and once on my fingers." She swung her boots. "I keep waiting for it to *feel* like it happened. At Fenmark, when a house qualified, they rang the bell in the yard. Somebody climbed up and rang it until his arms gave out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:219:"Had it off a carter, back along," said the innkeeper. "Paid a whole mark for it. I read it out to every guest who'll stand still." He put a finger on the glass and read it out now, in a ringing voice, while the bags stood dripping on his floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:29:The broadsides had been pricing the bout for two days, and Cael had seen them pasted on the walls of the delegation quarter all the way in, one on top of another where the bill-stickers had run out of wall. Some were about Auremont's perfect product and some about the girl Fenmark had thrown out. The best of them simply printed the two names side by side in large type and left the reader to do the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:295:Every person in that hall who could read the board knew it. Zerin had posted a Silver figure in an Iron semifinal, as plainly as the Standard could say anything. And two places below it, two points short, stood a girl whom Fenmark's ledger had once written down as Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:25:"Stone. All of them. They warm up for an hour because the floors are cold, and they never stand still between exchanges, and they've never in their lives been told to walk a circle. They just do." She looked at him sideways. "You'd have saved a shoulder if you'd been born up there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:19:"Rich." She turned the boot and started on the heel. "We used to be four people round one table with a clause and a lot of opinions. Now look. Our house is going to top these standings tomorrow; I've seen the board. The regional champion of the upper Iron is sitting at our table doing his homework." Ephram kept writing, but his ears went red. "And the strangest of us has his name chalked up on boards in towns he's never been to, before his wagon's even stopped." She held the boot up to the candle and looked along it. "I keep waiting for the bill. I've been waiting for it all week. That's Fenmark, that is. Fenmark taught me that anything good comes with a bill behind it." She put the boot down. "Karis says if I can catch myself at it, I'm halfway out of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:"You are," said Karis, writing. "And it'll come. Things like that always send one." She turned a page. "The difference between now and Fenmark is solvency."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:Nobody had read it, and nobody needed to. Withrow's runner had gone from her door at a run, which runners on the bluff did not do, and had knocked at four other doors in turn. Four people had come out of those doors and crossed the second quadrangle toward the records hall with the particular walk of people who have been asked to bring nothing and come now. Bracken was one, and Gault another, carrying a folder. Rooke came last, with his hands in the pockets of his dark coat, and stopped in the middle of the quadrangle to say something to a cohort boy who ran off toward the halls. By the time the lecture range let out, the second-years were telling one another it was a death in the chancellor's family, and the third-years were telling one another it was money. A Current boy on the lecture stair was telling anybody who would stand still that it was war.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:221:Lira went first, because she could not stand still. She ran the northern triangle from end to end and then the southern, at three-quarter pace, with the heat-wrap gone from her left arm for the first time in a week and the arm carried a little close. When she came back to the west gate she was hardly breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-38.md:145:He was at the far end of the long table with the observation notebook shut on the table before him and his bad arm resting in its loose linen across his lap. He had not seen her for two days, and it had taken him until Friday afternoon to notice, which embarrassed him once he had. Since the autumn Karis had kept time like the water clock: every session, every meal, every evening in the stable, and the archive so regularly that the bells might have been set by her. Then, from Brom's room on the night of the match, nothing. The only sighting was Brom's: Saturday at first light, a figure crossing the yard at speed with the collar up, gone before he could call out. When Cael asked Lira, Lira said only, "She's writing something she doesn't want watched." Lira would know. Lira had once lost two days of her own to four paragraphs from Fenmark, and he did not ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:253:"Mine. The one I didn't get." She sat back. "Same Path. Same sort of start. Somebody looked at each of us young and thought there was something there. Fenmark put me in a ledger. Auremont put her in a program. Then Fenmark threw me out, and I went and made myself out of whatever was lying about. A ledger-keeper at Ardenmere. A circuit full of fair-day men. You. Brom. A broom in the common room." She almost laughed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:165:"Fenmark used to talk about it," she said. "The older girls who'd been. They'd tell it at supper, the year before a cycle, how big it was and how the floors rang and how the crowds sounded. Every year they told it, and every year it got bigger." She was looking at the road ahead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:213:They ran it four more times before the light went, and she won three of them. He ran those on his feet alone, with one burst left that he meant to keep. The one she lost was the last, when Cael stopped trying to reach the middle at all and simply stood still in the open, a little off centre, and let her come. She could not price a road he did not mean to take. She stood ten feet from him for most of a minute with her hands on her hips, and then laughed and called it cheating, and said that Zerin would never do it, because Zerin did not know how to stand still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:85:She had seen him stand still. She did not know why, and she had decided not to wait to find out. She laid the crease from the oak straight down the long fall to the north barrier, to the place where he stood with the mast at his shoulder, and came down it, with the fire at its head. It was clean and honest and final, and every line out of that corner ran into something she had already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:115:She stood at the foot of her own lane, by the north mast, where the fire had gone into the iron. In all her public bouts nobody had ever seen her stand still in the middle of an exchange except once on a trial floor, for a second. She looked first at the barrier where he had been. Then along the foot of it, to the scorch his little fire had left at the end of his lane. Then at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:He had been thinking about the full push since the day the half came. The half had come not from reaching harder but from choosing a wider road before the force arrived, a channel cut for twice the water. A whole slow push would need the whole road, as broad as Brom's arm, laid out back along the line the push would come in and held there, steady, the whole time the force was arriving. If he reached for more than he had chosen, or chose the whole and then flinched from the size of it, it would go into a joint. The size had to be decided first and then not changed, and that was the entire difficulty, because nothing in him wanted to stand still while a thing that large came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:Nobody ever had. In a year of wardens and evaluators and brass plates and panels, everybody who looked at him had been looking for a line to fill in or a figure to post. Nothing in her face was going to be written down anywhere. She meant to find out what he was before she went on, because he was on none of her tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:25:"Watch him between exchanges," she said. "He won't stand still. None of them do. They train on stone, and stone's cold, and nobody in those hills has stood still between two exchanges since they could walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:47:"He's not going for the door," said Lira, at the rail. "He's using it. Every time Brom shuts it, he has to stand still to do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:337:Every one of their chairs had a brass plate on its back with a name cut into it. He had read the plates from the floor with a borrowed glass on the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:349:*Every other chair in that row has a brass plate on its back. Twelve has a pale square where a plate used to be, two empty screw holes, a brushed seat, and a steward at the head of the stair. No name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:105:The Fenmark boy was on the floor first. He was tall and narrow and careful, with a long reach and a face that was trying hard to show nothing. He was a Wind Path, like Lira, because Wind was what that academy was famous for; it was why a Wind girl of twelve had been sent there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:119:Cael knew it from Lira's descriptions, though he had never seen it run. It was the Fenmark way of taking a centre: a measured walk forward and then a burst at a fixed distance, then a reset and a second burst at the same distance, so that the fighter arrived in the middle of the floor having spent two bursts and owning the space between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:167:It was the Fenmark sequence, the four forms run one into the next: the first to take the centre, the second to press, the third to turn the opponent, the fourth to finish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:169:It was the thing Fenmark taught its Wind fighters across their whole time there, one form for every year a student stayed, and a fighter who could run all four cleanly was a fighter Fenmark would put its name to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:Lira had been taught that turn at Fenmark at fourteen. She had told them, at fourteen, that it was wrong. The right foot was the one to plant, she had said; on the left, there was half a beat in the middle of the turn when the fighter was neither pressing nor guarding, when his weight was all on one leg and that leg was the wrong one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:211:The fourth form was the finish, and it was the hardest thing in the Fenmark book. It was a third-year piece at the earliest, and most fighters did not run it cleanly until they were leaving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:341:"That's Fenmark," said Lira. "That's it exactly. You could put that over their door." She finished her stew and pushed the bowl away. "The boy was all right, though. He laughed when I told him. He didn't have to laugh. He had a whole hall of his own people watching him lose to the girl they threw out, and he laughed." She considered it. "I'd have liked him at fourteen, I think. I hope somebody up there is kind to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain board of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:195:"Mine is Zerin," she said. "I lost to her over four exchanges with the whole city watching, and I've been living on what that bought me ever since. Best thing that's happened to me since Fenmark put my things outside the door." She came away from the window and sat on the end of the bench. "And you've spent a year being measured by things that can't take your weight. Plates and panels and tables and men with rods. There's one person in this city whose measure of you would be worth having, and this morning she asked for it with one word." She looked at him. "I said somebody had to argue against. I never said it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-29.md:69:"You told me that. About Fenmark, the night of the annex."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:73:"And friendly isn't a safe word from them," said Lira. "I've been asked into a friendly room by an institution. At Fenmark, the morning they told me I was finished there. Everybody was very kind." She folded her arms. "It was a very kind room, and I walked out of it with nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:113:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:43:He walked. He did not chase her and he did not stand still. He went round the floor at his own pace on a circle of his own, and the circle kept bringing him to her whether she liked it or not, so that her waiting turned, slowly, into being found. He touched her twice in the second half of the exchange, both plain drives through the middle when her step to the side came a fraction short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:"Fenmark threw you away?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:169:"I used to want them to admit what I already was." A coal shifted in the grate. "The ledger. Fenmark. The registry. I wanted somebody with a stamp to write down the thing I knew, so that I could stop having to show it to people." She was quiet for a moment. "Now I want to become something they haven't seen yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:Cael could not hear all of it from the bench, but he could see it, and he could read Lira standing at an official table with her arms folded better than he could read most people's faces. Her clerk was a grey-haired woman with spectacles on a cord. Lira's file had come up from Greyvane ahead of her: the old Fenmark record, the acknowledgment of standing she had signed before any of them knew her, and, pinned at the back, the sheet Greyvane had given her. It was the flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, under Greyvane's seal, the provost's whole two terms of watching folded into one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-58.md:233:Cael felt the quiet all round him, and three hundred people inside it waiting to carry something home. He thought of the Log in his coat on the bench, open at last night's page. He thought of the line under *Evidence* with nothing after it but *Ruling*. He had filled that line in this afternoon, for the crossing. There was another line, under all of it, that had never had anything written beside it at all, since a word on a brass plate in Denvash, and there was nothing beside it now. Something in him had done a thing he could not describe. He had watched it happen from the inside, like a tenant watching the house he rents get up and walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:There had been a hall at Fenmark with a long table and a woman behind it who had spent about a minute and a half on her, and at the end of the minute and a half had written a word and a number: Copper, Rank One. That was three years ago. Since then Lira had fought on a foundry floor and on the Ironyard's planks and in a formal yard at Greyvane, and in front of eight hundred people here, and she had beaten fighters whose paper said Iron. She had a flag on her own file, put there by somebody who had once looked at her properly, and the flag had gone into a queue, and the queue was where it still was. And every morning at the fifth bell the clerk chalked the same word beside her name as he had chalked the morning she arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:55:*Three men told me the same thing inside a day, in three different rooms, and none of them asked the others first. Gault at the long table: my wing changes nothing. Rooke under the north arch: keep doing it, it's as boring as it sounds. Bracken over his counter the next morning: give me a dull month. Not one of the three is fond of the other two. One instruction between them. Hold the ordinary. Stand still while they come.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:117:"I asked my aunt once what an Archmarshal was. She's an assessor, at the Fenmark station. She said she'd met one in thirty years, at a dinner, and he'd asked her to pass the salt, and she'd never been so frightened of a sentence in her life." Gwen looked at him with frank interest across the table. "Are you frightened?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:"He isn't a friend," she said. "He isn't even a stranger. He's a man who was offered a job, and the job is Cael, and he said yes. Nobody carried him up the bluff road in a sack. Any morning he likes, he could walk down to the ferry and go home, and every morning he gets up instead and goes to stand wherever he can do the most damage. On the circuit at Fenmark, if a man came at you with a thing, you didn't owe it him back. You took it off him if you could. Nobody there would have called that stealing. They'd have called it learning to stay alive."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:31:"It's too tidy." She was frowning at the table's edge. "Since Seventh-day I've had it going round in my head, round and round, and it shrinks a little on every lap. When I'm right about a fighter the page gets longer every time I look at it; I keep finding more. This keeps shrinking. Something drops out of it every time, and I can't see what." She rolled one shoulder against the warmth. "Write that down. Not the Fenmark part. The shrinking part."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:He had spent a week after the back bench trying to make the read last while Brom moved, because he wanted it to last; he had sat a whole card and a whole sweeping still enough to feel somebody at the far edge of a room. Somewhere under that he had begun to want, very badly, a version of the thing that would be worth something on a floor, against a man who did not stand still to be found. So every morning in the alcove he opened the door sitting, and Brom stood up, and took one slow step, and the door went out. He opened it again, and Brom lifted a hand, and it went out. Brom walked a slow circle round him at three paces and it went out on the first stride, every time, forty times, sixty, while the band came and went and came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-09.md:107:Cael thought about Pellin's office, the circle of pale wood, the brass plate that lit eleven seconds late. He thought about a form drawn from the back of a drawer, filled in with small quick strokes, and how nobody in that room had asked him anything, because there had not been anything to ask; the question had already been answered by someone who was not in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:15:"No. I mean it's small." She hitched the strap higher. "Fenmark had a hall. A great tall window you could see from the far end of the square, and a hall behind it where they kept you waiting in rows like loaves. There were forty of us. Every time an assessor came through the far door we all stood up, and every time he went out again we all sat down." Her voice carried none of it. That was how Cael knew the weight. "I stood up nine times that morning before they got to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:129:At Fenmark she had been fourteen and frightened and nobody had told her anything. She had put her hand where a clerk pointed, and something had come and looked at her for about ninety breaths, and then a man who had not once lifted his head from his page had written *Copper* and *Rank One*, and that had been her. She had not cried until she was outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:She had thought, on the road down, that when she heard it she would feel something enormous, and that she would have to stand very still to keep it from showing. She did not feel anything enormous. She felt the cold of the frame going out of her palm a little at a time, and a small sore place at the back of her heel where her boot had rubbed on the bluff road, and the bench-hard ache in the hip. Ninety breaths at Fenmark had made her Copper. Four seconds in a grain factor's counting room had unmade it. It seemed to her that there ought to be more to it than that, and that there was not, and that this was exactly right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:It took ten. A clerk came in from the front office with a second book, thicker than the first, and sat at the end of the table, and entered the supersession in it, line by line, with a pen that scratched. While he wrote, the woman explained to Lira that her Fenmark certificate would not be destroyed. It would remain in the registry as a historical entry, marked superseded, so that the line of her record was unbroken, and any reader in future would be able to see where she had stood before and on what date she had been moved. The prior classification had to be superseded on the same instrument that made the new one, the woman said, or it stayed live underneath, and that caused trouble later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:Cael had looked at it every time he came into this room and had never once got a name for it. Squat, copper, three short legs, a little larger than two fists: that was the pot. Out of its lid stood a glass straw scored with lines as close as a comb's teeth. A small brass plate on its belly carried a date of calibration in paint, and the date was this month's. The clerk set it on its legs on the oak a pace from the crossing and stepped back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:31:Fenmark came back to her while she was doing up the second boot, as it had come back most mornings this week. The station hall with its long bench, and the cold coming up through the flagstones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:215:She thought about the station hall at Fenmark, and the clerk's pen. She thought about Fiske on the oil ten days ago, and the honest thing Fiske had said afterward across the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:255:And that alone was not a finding, and he would not let it become one. A man might stand that still from long habit. He might stand still because the cold had got into his joints and moving hurt. He might simply have decided to, for reasons of his own. Cael wrote all three on the page and could not choose between them, so he wrote *stood still* under them and left it there, unexplained, which was the honest thing to do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:"I know a clerk at Fenmark who never once looked up from his page." She said it without any heat. "He didn't mean me harm. He meant to be tidy. That's how they do it, mostly; they tidy you." She held his eyes. "Be the fighter. In a measured room. Both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:"He won't mind that. He'll like it, if you show him." She went to the door. "Don't show him tonight. His head still hurts." And then, with her hand on the latch, not looking round: "The last one's right. The question. I've been asking it since Fenmark, only I never had it in a sentence." She went out, and shut the door very quietly behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:She did something else. She went straight at him, and halfway there went right, short and quick, and the right was real too, and his held-back weight was held back on the wrong side. *Two bursts.* The touch landed on his leading shoulder from behind and to the side while the landing beat still had her by the collar. She had used the beat as a place to stand still and reach from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-19.md:253:And it was not silence. He knew that now, standing in the yard, as clearly as he had known it in the side room. Silence would have meant something there, choosing to keep quiet, as Pellin's brass plate had kept dark for eleven seconds before it lit. He could have waited out a silence. He could have knocked louder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:"Fiske told me to make them look." She said it slowly, laying the words out one at a time to see how they sat together. "Win the crown, she said, and then make them look at you. The crown's only how you force it. I've been thinking about that for a month. I thought she meant the registry. Somebody at a desk in Fenmark with my flag in a queue under forty other flags." She turned her head on the floor and looked at him. "And now the most senior pair of eyes in the whole Compact is coming up our road on its own feet, and it's going to be sitting in the gallery on the nineteenth whether I like it or not, and it isn't coming for me at all. It's coming for you. I'm what happens to be on the boards while it's here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:For two years everybody who had looked hard at Cael had been looking for the place where he would break. Coss had looked carefully, and the registry had looked carefully, but the careful looking had been aimed at him, the way a smith aims a hammer, and he had learned to stand still under it and say only true things. He realized, sitting at the long table, that he had never once until now been looked at that carefully on his own behalf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:Lira did not say what hers was. They all knew. Her papers would travel south ahead of her, the old ones from Fenmark and the new one from this hill. The new one was the best thing Greyvane had been able to give her, and Greyvane had never had the weight to make anybody act on it: a flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, signed by a provost who had watched her put Iron-equivalent fighters on the floor for two whole terms. It was the best Greyvane could do. Everybody at the table knew it was not enough. Her formal standing still said *Copper, Rank Two*, and a ladder read off formal standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:123:"Fenmark," she said, out of nowhere. "When I was fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:125:He waited. She had told him about Fenmark once, on a road, in the dark, and never again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-36.md:33:At the fourth bell he went up to his room and wrapped his forearms in guard-cloth, both arms, wrist to elbow, wound firm and tied off with his teeth as he had done it a hundred times in Ardenmere. He flexed the hands. He put the binder in his coat and took it out again, because a binder was no use in a ring. He held it, and then went down the corridor and knocked on Lira's door and gave it to her. She took it without a word and put it inside her own coat, against her ribs, on the side where she kept Fenmark's four paragraphs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-04.md:179:"I've been carrying this as if Fenmark invented it just for me. As if they'd looked at me and decided, specially, that I was the problem." She set the cup down. "It turns out there's a desk for it. A queue. Other people standing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:55:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:123:"I thought it would be appropriate." She took out a notebook with a cover she had decorated herself and opened it to a clean page, on which she wrote the date, and underlined it twice, and then wrote *FIELD ASSESSMENT* in capitals and underlined that twice as well. "My aunt's an assessor at the Fenmark station. She says it's the only work left where they pay you to look at people and nobody minds. Do you think we'll learn to be invisible?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:171:Gwen walked out with Cael as far as the covered walk, talking the whole way. Her aunt at Fenmark had said assessors were born, not made, but her aunt had also said that about pastry, and her aunt's pastry was terrible. She had wanted to be an assessor since she was seven. Was it true that the assay provision meant he could never lose a bout? Somebody in the Current hall had said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:127:The fourth was the first one with the right idea in it. It gave up the man altogether and drew the bluff, every hall and passage and stair as a box with a letter in it, so that each sighting could be filed under its room. It took him two evenings. And it was wrong in a way he could not see until he had poured three days of shorthand into the boxes and found the shorthand would not go in. The man was never in a room. He was always on the edge of one. Rails, landings, the top of a stair, the middle of a knot of five people on a tier: the seams between one space and the next, where a person can stand still for a long time without anybody feeling that he has stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Nothing moved. That was all, and every bit of it was hard. A building full of frightened people, waiting for eleven strangers and a man with no stated business, did not want to stand still. It wanted to sweep its passages and re-ink its ledgers and move its awkward things out of sight. Cael saw the want everywhere. He saw a Stone instructor stop in the second quadrangle with a broom she had picked up from somewhere, and look at it, and look at the flagstones, and then lean the broom carefully back against the wall where she had found it and walk away with her hands in her pockets. He saw two clerks from the bursar's office arguing in low voices at the foot of the lecture stair about whether the lamp-oil ledger ought to be copied fair before the twelfth. Then one of them said *posted day*, and the other said *posted day*, and they went back up the stair together without copying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-48.md:281:The category was not for anybody whose classification could not be read. It was for people who did not have one. Section four gave an assessor the power to put a practitioner into the category, and section one, three pages earlier, said who could be in it. A boy whose Arbiter had returned nothing would be in it. A boy whose return had been lost, or never recorded, would be in it. And a boy whose Arbiter had taken eleven seconds and then returned a word, a real word, a word with four instances and a statute of its own in the Supplementary Register, and a brass plate that had lit eleven seconds late, had a primary classification. He had more of one than anybody in the district. It simply was not one the standard framework could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:157:Lira put her hand inside her coat and took out the envelope with the green wax on it, which he knew. She had carried it there since the ninth week, against her chest, with the four paragraphs inside that a man at Fenmark had signed without ever meeting her. She did not open it. She slid a finger under the flap and drew out, from behind the folded sheet, a smaller slip of paper, folded once and soft at the edges, as if it had been taken out and put back a good many times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:"You said it on this wall," said Lira. "The night I'd read the four paragraphs and decided they didn't get to tell me who I was. You'd had it off Vell, and you turned it round and gave it to me. I wrote it down that night so I'd have it the way we'd made it, not the way I'd have made it alone." She looked at the slip in his hand. "I've had it behind Fenmark ever since. Every time I took the four paragraphs out to read them again, which I did more than I'd ever tell you, that came out first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-34.md:165:"At Fenmark," she said, "I knew the right foot for the third turn, and I said so, and they wrote me down in nineteen words and put me out." She was looking at the ring. "I've spent two years knowing things about floors that nobody let me teach. I taught you, in Ardenmere, but I didn't know I was doing it. You were just a boy in a yard I was trying to keep alive." She shifted on the stone. "This is the first time I've stood on a floor and known exactly what I was doing to somebody, and why, and that it was right. Nobody asked me to. Nobody's going to sign it or write it in a ledger. There's no panel." She almost smiled. "It's the best teaching I've ever done, and it's in a frozen yard with a sack of peas, and the only people who'll ever know are you and Brom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:185:She came straight, as she had come at the end of their first bout: one plain heavy delivery, built and committed, the Force that every other fighter on the sheet used, coming across her own slick on feet that knew where the oil lay. There was nowhere to go from it. Lira could not step back; behind her was slick. She could not step aside; slick. She could burst, and land, and stand still in front of the one woman on the bluff who hit still things best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:249:"You were going to say something kind and sensible, and I'd have had to agree with it." She kept her eyes on the dot. "Here's the trouble. For two years I've told anyone who'd stand still that Fenmark got me wrong. Then I walked in here and put my name to a page that says Fenmark's record stands, and I did it so I could train, and now there's a mark by my name every morning to remind everyone that my wins need a second signature." She breathed out through her nose. "I was right about Fenmark. I also signed. Both of those are true, and I can't make one of them sit down and let the other have the chair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-43.md:233:That was the thing she had not let herself see until tonight, sitting on a cold floor with the backs of her hands going dark. Her card said *Wind, Copper*, the way it had said it since before Fenmark, and under that a line from Fenmark she had never been shown. Nothing that happened on Vell's floor would ever change a word of it. Vell could write *Iron-equivalent* in letters a foot high, and Lira could put a Bronze on her knee every Thursday until the river froze, and the examiner at Fenmark would never see it, because the examiner did not read Vell's book. Nobody across the river read Vell's book. To move the card she would need an Arbiter, and a guild to send her to one, and no guild in the city had answered her in a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-43.md:243:The card would say what it said, and Fenmark would think what it thought. She still wanted them to think otherwise. She had wanted it too long to stop wanting it on one cold floor, and she did not pretend to herself that she had. But wanting a thing and walking to a gate to beg for it were not the same, and the one thing in all of it that would change because she worked at it was her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-43.md:265:"I'm going to tell you one thing, so it's said." There was a pause. "I'm not asking Dace for another. Not till the middle's gone. Vell's right, and I'd rather be provisional and true than the other thing on a lucky night." Another pause. "And I'm done walking to their gate. The guilds. Fenmark. I still want what's behind it; I'm only done asking. If they want to know, they can come and read the book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-31.md:163:The frost had come back after two days of rain, and the top of the wall was rough with it under his hand. Below them the hill town's lamps were coming on down the slope, along the street worth walking and then the street worth avoiding. On the corner stood the grey building with its board over the door, where Oona would go down in a little under six weeks with her mother's hand on her shoulder. Lira had her knees drawn up and her coat round her. Fenmark's four paragraphs, he knew without seeing them, were folded inside it against her ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:65:Her clerk was an older man with half-moon spectacles and the slow, careful manner of someone who had handled difficult paper before, and he did not hurry. From a box at his elbow he took a thin file, and it took Cael a moment to understand that it was her Fenmark file. It had arrived here before she had, because academies talked to one another about the students they had thrown out. The clerk laid it open beside a printed sheet of rules and compared the two line by line, moving a finger down each in turn, making a small mark now and then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:81:From where he stood, Cael could read the heading whether he wanted to or not. *Acknowledgment of Prior Standing.* The clerk read it in a flat, even voice: that Fenmark Academy had ended her enrollment; that the record of that ending was to stand as written; that her candidacy for re-certification at Greyvane was granted on the understanding that she accepted that record as accurate and did not dispute it as a condition of entry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:107:"I know." She breathed out. "I could have marked it and fought about it later. I could have stood there and told that nice man exactly why Fenmark was wrong; I've had the speech ready for two years." She turned her head and looked at him. "I'd rather have a bed in this place than be proved right on paper about what Fenmark did to me at fourteen. That's new, for me. Wanting the one thing more than the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:"I was ready for it to be Fenmark all over again. That day, standing in a room while somebody reads you what you are." She looked down at her hands. "It doesn't. It feels like setting a pack down at the end of a road. I didn't know how much it weighed until it was on the ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-02.md:305:*Brom got through intake in eleven minutes and didn't know what to do with himself. Lira signed a paper that said Fenmark was right about her, and she says it felt like putting something down. I'm not sure yet what it felt like for me. I'll tell you when I know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Fenmark," Lira said at last, to the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:She saw it before she saw Quenna, which annoyed her, because she had meant to walk in and look at the person first like a civilised human being. It was long and stiff and the colour of weak tea, with her name on the front in a clerk's round hand. On the back was a blob of green wax with Fenmark's crest pressed into it. She had last seen that crest at the top of a letter telling a twelve-year-old girl from the river quarter that she had been awarded a place. Green wax. She had forgotten the green. She had not forgotten anything else about the place, and somehow the green had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:"That's Fenmark's record of the finding." Quenna did not touch the envelope; she only looked at it. "What came here with you at intake was the notice that your enrollment had ended, and your standing record. That was enough to put you on the track. It isn't enough for a formal review. The re-certification provision says the panel must have the originating academy's full record of the finding before it sits, so I wrote to them for it three weeks ago, when your review went on the calendar." A pause, very small. "They took their time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:225:The man at the end of the table had made the sentence up. She held that thought in her hand on the stair like a stone she had found and decided to keep. Fenmark had not written it down. Fenmark had not even thought it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:255:*Fenmark's record came. Lira read it in the annex with Quenna and me at the door. Four paragraphs, one sheet. She came out lighter than she went in. I don't know what's in it. She'll tell me Thursday, after the floor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:79:"There's a file about me at Fenmark," she said finally. "I've never read it. But I know one word in it, because they read it aloud to me on the day they threw me out. *Insubordination.* And the man who read it said, so that I'd be sure to understand, that it meant I had no control of myself." She set the flask on the rail. "So when I take a hit now, I take it where everyone can see I chose to. The ones I slip, nobody remembers. The ones I take on purpose, they remember I didn't have to." She looked at Karis directly for the first time. "That isn't for your notebook."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-41.md:169:Lira looked at her from the wall, with an expression Cael had seen on her face once before, on the morning she had read four paragraphs from Fenmark and decided they did not get to say who she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-10.md:181:"I know a bit about being written down." Her voice dropped. "Fenmark kept a file on me. It's sitting in a box at the intake desk right now, and I've never once opened it. I know who wrote it, though. And I know that not one of them ever asked me a single thing first. They watched me for a year and wrote down what they'd decided they saw, and then a clerk stood in a room and read it to me, and that was who I was." She let out a breath. "So somebody says *I'd like to study you*, and up go my shoulders before I can stop them. That isn't me arguing with her. It's a scar, and I know a scar from an argument. I'm pointing at it so you know it's there. Later on, when my face does something about her, you'll know where it's coming from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-27.md:69:"No." Lira did not take her eyes off the gate. "Nobody asked Quenna to write to Fenmark three weeks early, either. Nobody asked her to put the envelope on the table and tell me it was a version of a choice. She just did it, because it was there to be done." She turned her head and looked at him. "On Monday I found out that nobody ever argued for me. Not one person, not once, in a room I spent two years filling with people. I'd like to find out what it's like to be the one who does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-42.md:163:She said nothing, but he watched her face go back two years, to a girl being handed nineteen words from Fenmark that had already been used against her before anyone let her read them. It was the face of someone who had learned early how far the machine reached above any office with a door you could knock on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:69:She had spent two hits in that exchange, and she had spent them without a flicker, the way a person counts out coins she came intending to spend and does not stop to look at them on the way across the counter. That was the whole of her argument, Cael understood, standing at the rail with his hands tight on the cold wood. She was not trying to win cleanly. She was showing three people with the Fenmark sheet at their elbows how far past a Copper ceiling she could go, and what she would pay to go there. She would pay it in front of them without looking down at the bill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:101:He knew what it was. He had spent his whole life inside records and he knew what it was, and he felt it go through him in the cold yard like a draught under a door. It did not change her tier. She was Copper on Fenmark's sheet and Copper on Greyvane's ledger and she would be Copper on the board this evening with the dot still beside her name. It did not reverse anything, or retract anything, or call anyone wrong. It only stated, in an academy's own language, initialed by three people and dated, that the recorded tier and the floor did not agree. For two years the only paper in the world about Lira had been four paragraphs written by somebody who had never watched her fight. Now there were two pieces of paper, and the second one, written by three people who had, said in plain ink that the first did not add up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:213:"That they were wrong." She said it with no heat in it at all. "I'm not filing a dispute. Not now. Maybe not ever. This morning's line does more for me here than any argument with Fenmark would, and I'd have to stand up in front of more people who were never in the room." She patted the coat. "But I'm keeping these. For the day the proving runs the other way, and some office owes me an admission instead of me owing it proof."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-26.md:231:*Lira's review. Two bouts, two won. She spent two hits in the second like coins and finished with the barrow turn from the Ardenmere market yard. The notation, read aloud, in their words: Advancement inconsistent with recorded tier. Recommend continued track with priority review. First paper anywhere that says her file might be wrong. She's keeping Fenmark's four paragraphs. No dispute.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-37.md:223:"It's better to fight somebody you like." She stood up off the stair and stretched her back until it cracked. "You don't do anything stupid to them. You don't try to make them look small, or hit them where it doesn't count because you're angry. You fight the fight and not the man." She looked at the cloth on her wrist. "The worst bouts I ever had at Fenmark were against girls I couldn't stand. I spent the whole of every one trying to win twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:Lira went still. Cael saw her shoulders start to come up, and saw her not let them, and knew what she was hearing. It was every instructor at Fenmark who had ever told her that a step was wrong because it was not the step in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-46.md:37:"Bede counted two," she said. "He sat on a bench all season with a clerk's book and counted two. Do you think a man who's had masters paid to stand behind him since he was ten can't count two?" She took the staff away. "Your hip's a door. You go through it, and then you have to stand still in the doorway while it shuts behind you. That's the shape of it; you wrote it down yourself. Against him you go through that door when you know. When you've read him and the knock's told you and there's nobody at the other side. Not because you're frightened."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:45:"I'm going," she said again, to hear how it sounded the second time. It sounded the same. "I knew it on the stone last night, before she'd finished saying *Fenmark*. It was there this morning when I woke, sitting on the end of my bed like a cat. I lit the lamp tonight to make sure I wasn't only saying it because I was frightened, or because you'd go anyway and I didn't want to be left on this hill on my own." She turned her head and looked at him at last. "I'm not. I went up there and asked my feet. They said yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:85:Cael answered the ones he could, which were few. Lira answered one or two more, from Fenmark. At Fenmark, she said, a standard student came in at fourteen or fifteen and stayed four years, if he was not sent home; the town round an academy always had work for anybody with a strong back and no pride, carrying and kitchens and the stables; and every academy she had ever heard of kept somebody on its staff whose whole business was reading. "They'd not have a reader like you," she said to Brom. "Nobody has a reader like you. But they'll have somebody who'll know what you are when you stand in front of him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-40.md:5:It was very well done, and Cael did not see it for what it was until the second time. Keth came forward at a walk, as he had in the first, and chained a two from the end of his reach, and at the end of the second cut he was slow bringing the blade back. It was not much, a hand's width of lag, a line left open across his body for a moment longer than a man as quick as Keth should ever have left it. It looked like a tired arm, and two exchanges at his own speed against a boy who would not stand still might have tired anybody's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-32.md:137:"Fenmark never once wrote an expulsion as good as this waiver," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:283:So he told her. He told her about the shelves, and the beam, and the weight that moved and did not stand still, and *something I've never seen before*. He told it as nearly as he could in Brom's own words, because he did not want to make them into his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-33.md:35:"It only said yes and no," Brom agreed. "That's what it says when you walk. The rest you have to stand still for." He turned the loaf over and looked at the crust. "It'll say more, later. But you've got to let it say the small thing well first, or it'll never trust you with the big one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-07.md:111:"I do," she said. "I do drop left. I didn't know." She looked at him. "I learned it from a man in a barn when I was fourteen. Before the academy. He was the first one who ever showed me anything. He used to tie his left hand behind his back to teach, so his right would do the showing, and it never worked, he always forgot and untied it." She laughed, not quite properly. "He was left-handed. Everything he did, he did from the left. And I copied him for a year in that barn before anybody at Fenmark ever saw me. And nobody at Fenmark ever said. Not once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-11.md:17:Her body did not believe her. That was the thing nobody told you. You could decide a thing on a stone floor at midnight with your back to a wall, decide it all the way down. And then you stood at a mark with a room round you, and your knees and your hands and the long muscles of your back had not been at that conversation. They had been at Fenmark, and they remembered the examination hall, and three examiners, and a slate, and the long walk out with her face giving them nothing. They remembered the back stairs where the coal went up, and the third step from the bottom, and coming apart there in the dark where nobody could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-11.md:67:There was a way to take nothing, and she did not have it. There was a way to take all of it, and she would not. In the half a breath she had she turned. She did not turn away, which was too late, but into it, the way she had turned into the senior student's chop at Fenmark a lifetime ago. The blow that should have gone in under her guard and folded her met the long muscle along her ribs instead, and the hard edge of her elbow, and spent most of itself there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-11.md:151:"So did I. Before I asked Dace." She stood holding the rail and getting her breath back round the side. "It comes out at about a fifth of my rent a week, if I lose half of them. More if I lose more." She looked at him. "I'd like to see anybody at Fenmark tell me what I learned tonight is worth less than a fifth of my rent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-11.md:229:"I am." He tried to find the reason, and found that it was not a reason he had worked out but a thing he had seen. "After Fenmark she asked six guilds for a letter. She walked to two of the halls on her own feet. The first one wouldn't let her in the door." He watched Lira's hands on the staff. "She went to the second one anyway. She knew what the first had said. She went anyway, so she'd have heard it from both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-50.md:15:He did not know, when he said it, where he was going. He knew only that he could not stand still at the top of the fish steps with the post at his back and say the rest of it, not yet, not with the door that stuck still swinging behind him. His legs knew where they were going before he did. They took him up away from the river and across the corner of the food market, past the bread woman's high cracked lantern and the cheese stall's dark one, and up the narrowing street toward the yards. It was not until he saw the black square of the Cinder House sign at the corner two streets below the lane that he understood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-41.md:27:*Not taking. It isn't all there, and it isn't mine the way Lira's Path is hers. She was declared at Kindling and it's been hers every day since, whole, to use when she likes. What I've got is more like the outline of how she moves, without the inside. It comes properly when something's coming at me and I haven't got time to choose. When I ask for it on purpose, slowly, by the cot, it comes too, but badly. It's like copying out a word in somebody else's handwriting. You can see whose hand it was meant to be. Nobody would mistake it for theirs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"He looked in," she said. "The young one. On the way past the big door. I was making a noise on the post the way Dace likes, and he stopped in the door and looked at me for about as long as it takes to say *good morning*, and then he went on." She put the bread down. "He looked at me like I was weather. Like I was a girl hitting a sack, which I was." She was quiet a moment. "I'm in their book too, you know. Same as you. *Wind, Copper*, and under it whatever Fenmark wrote when they sent me home. I've never seen it. I don't want to." She picked the bread up again and did not eat it. "When I first came down from the academy I couldn't walk past a collar pin without my hands going cold. For a whole winter. I used to cross the street."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:"I used to think," said Lira, a mile on, "that the best day of my life would be the day a letter came from Fenmark. Saying they'd been wrong. Saying the examiner had been wrong, and the hall had been wrong, and they'd like me to come back, and they were sorry." She walked a few paces. "I used to lie awake writing it for them. I had it word for word. I'd changed it a hundred times."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-60.md:121:"Now I don't think it'll ever come, and I find I don't need it to." She said it without any weight. "If it came, I'd read it. I'd probably keep it. I'd probably show it to you and make you read it twice. But I'd not need it. I don't know when that stopped. Somewhere on that hill. Somewhere between Maud and the lamp." She looked at the road ahead. "Greyvane's not about them any more. It was, when she first said *Fenmark* on the stone. It isn't now. It's about finding out how far it goes, whatever it is I've got, with somebody asking every month and me answering."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-60.md:127:"Oh, yes." Lira swung her staff up onto her shoulder. "I've not stopped wanting that for a minute. I've only stopped needing anybody at Fenmark to stand and watch me do it." She grinned at the road. "Give me a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-13.md:23:She stayed there, and stayed, and he counted it on his own breath, sitting still: five, six, seven. He had never seen a build go past four. Every fighter he had watched, of every Path, gathered whatever they were gathering and then committed it, because a gathered thing wanted to be spent. It was like holding a full bucket at arm's length; you could not do it for long. The Force woman held hers for seven counts, and then for nine, and she did not stand still to hold it but moved inside it, stepping and turning and following the lean man round the stone with the build sunk down in her legs and back, plain to see if you knew where to look. Once she took a light touch from him on the shoulder, a testing tap, and did not let go of any of it. The hooks went on coming out of Cael's pencil with longer and longer counts between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:203:He got as far as the gate in the wall, with Lira on his left side now, the rib side, without being asked. Then he had to stand still in the gateway and breathe, carefully, with his hand pressed flat against the catch, while people went past him out into the street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-54.md:207:"Wind Path, Copper-tier, expelled from Fenmark Academy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-21.md:37:He felt it want to, quite clearly, as though somebody had put a hand flat on his chest. A small cold current went up from his stomach through the middle of him and sat under his breastbone, and he knew the feeling, because he had last had it standing in a circle of pale wood on Weaver's Row while a brass plate failed to light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-10.md:31:"A guild. A sponsor's letter, with a seal on it, saying *this one's ours, see what she's become*." She rested her chin on her knees. "No guild in Valdris is going to put its seal on a letter for a girl Fenmark sent home. I know. I asked six of them, the year after. I walked to two of them. One let me into the hall and one didn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-10.md:59:"Nobody's asked me that since Fenmark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:215:It was not much: a big man's calm face, and for one instant the look in it of somebody in a lit room when every lamp goes out at once: not fear, not yet, only the blank start of a person whose world has just gone missing round him. Brom's guard was aimed at the stone a little to Cael's left. His weight had begun to go there to meet something, and it hung, half gone, stopped. Cael had seen the big man stand still for twelve days. He had never once seen him stuck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-01.md:147:She knew her own ledger by heart, and it was short. *Wind Path, Copper.* That was what the Arbiter had said when she was fourteen, and the registry had written it down. Nothing anyone did in a warehouse full of lamps would ever make it say anything else unless a guild sponsor put his name on a letter and sent her to an evaluation station to be looked at properly. And no guild in Valdris would put its name on a letter for a girl Fenmark Academy had sent home. She had found that out the year after, one door at a time. So she had a formal tier that would not move, and she had Vell's book, which would, if she gave it reasons. Copper-equivalent, high range, said Vell's book this month. She beat Iron-equivalents in practice twice a week, and practice did not count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-30.md:65:"Then when it does," said Lira, "tell me the same day. Not four days after." She began on the bread at last. "I'd rather know I've been heard than be kept from it. Being kept from things is what Fenmark did." She chewed, and swallowed, and pointed the end of the loaf at him. "And if you ever find out what I sound like when I'm cross, don't tell me that. I don't want to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:He wrote it up that night on the cot: the two mornings of Kestrel, the two failed nights on the wall, the half heartbeat in the grey half, as plainly as he could. Then he sat looking at what he had written, because none of it would stand still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:Pellin found the file, checked a line with her pen, and made a small mark. "Kindling assessment, the usual way. Your Arbiter wakes here, and the result goes straight to the registry ledger." She laid two fingers on a flat brass plate let into the desktop at her elbow, dull and unremarkable, about the size of a book. "When your result reaches the Registry, this lights, and I record what it shows. The resolution itself is usually done before you've finished a breath; with the paperwork, the whole appointment runs from three minutes to fifteen. Do you have questions first?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:91:He glanced at Pellin, who was three feet away watching the brass plate, and her face showed nothing, because she could not see it and nobody could; it was his, and it had always been his, and nobody else would ever see it. It was his Arbiter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:101:By now, he understood, the plate should have lit; Pellin had said it was usually done before you finished a breath, and he had finished one. He kept her face at the edge of his eye; her face was information. She was not looking at him but at the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:The brass plate at Pellin's elbow lit, eleven seconds late. Text rose up in it, pale and even, and settled into the same word he had just heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-23.md:201:What he needed was somebody who could not be beaten by lasting, because whoever it was would last better than he did. It had to be somebody who did not beat people so much as stand still while people beat themselves. Against somebody like that, a boy whose whole craft so far was to give ground and watch and survive would find, about the fifth exchange, that giving ground and watching and surviving were exactly what that fighter wanted him to do. Then he would have to do the other thing, or lose again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:The seal was dark wax pressed with the Compact's mark, a hand holding a balance, so cleanly that a machine must have done it. Cael thought, for no good reason, of the brass plate on Pellin's desk, and all that care poured into the outside of things. He broke the seal and unfolded the paper.

