editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:160:- Day 11 (ch 6): standing paid in standing; two outside the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:173:**Open threads now.** The entry demonstration date and what to put in the baseline (Karis's warning); Wray's re-measure; the timber floor's give; the low hall locked from outside; the five blank lines in the eight; Brom's silence about the east hall; Lira's registration; Karis's two registry offices; the inspection "sooner"; Rooke's dated objection; Vell's reply; Prynn's gap; the copy in the coat; Edran's third bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:183:- **Halcenvane details:** the toll board's nine lines (incl. a one-copper first-week enrollee line on the gate list); intake's eight shrouded desks; the library committee's eleven-clause access schedule with a written appeal to the chancellor; the timber east-hall floor; the low hall locked from outside with the key on a nail; seeded students get first pick of rooms; the porter chalks and polishes the crowns; the Copper Crown's plate riveted on twice; the crowns kept in a glass case in a long room on the first floor of the administration range; the clerk posts session minutes on the board by the theater door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:200:- Lira's Fenmark Arbiter reading took "about a minute and a half" — NEW CANON (no earlier book states it); the registrar's clerk "most of two minutes" (ch9).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:231:- **Lira's Kindling: "a minute and a half", Copper Rank One.** **Flag** against Books 1–2 of the edition.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:316:- **Lira's vote price** (above). Brom's pencil on the outside of the right wrap (the M2 ink stays inside the left).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:794:- **The delegation's card (flag):** officers do not converse with any party under review outside a sitting, do not acknowledge such a party "beyond ordinary courtesy", and do not carry messages, take papers or accept anything offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:856:- **Brom:** knee iced (her palm to the outside of the knee), knuckles of the right hand strapped (a long afternoon of her forearms), the shoulder sore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:910:- **Lira (flag, minor):** at her Kindling at Fenmark she "had not cried until she was outside". Fourteen is the source's age.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:193:He had handled more enrollment files than he could count, and this one was the best-made of any that had crossed his table in years. Each sheet carried one proof and said so in one line, and nothing argued, and nothing was ornamented. The delegation's counsel, who had wanted very badly to find a weak sheet, had found the load-bearing one in under a minute and a half on her first attempt and had looked, for a moment, as though she might clap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:He did not write that. He had no evidence for it. It lay a very long way outside his mandate. And an officer of his grade who puts a guess into a file has arranged for some junior man, three years on, to act on the guess without its history and without its doubt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:He did not say anything. At the table Lira had spent most of the afternoon not filling in a ladder form; she raised her eyes to Cael, and he gave the smallest shake of his head. Karis put her pen down; the room's other occupants, two Iron girls arguing about a lecture, went on arguing about it. Brom sat with his hands on his knees and his eyes shut and his breathing slow and even, and the clock on the mantel ticked through most of a minute and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:*Subject's routine is disciplined and unremarkable. Trains under supervision on the four allocated days, east hall timber and north hall stone, signed for by whichever instructor the office names; has not been seen on any floor outside those hours. Keeps the lecture pattern of an ordinary enrollee. Sits in the public tiers at every practice afternoon of the Crown yard, his associates' bouts and strangers' alike. Extensive private notation habit; two books, written in daily, the larger left in his room on training days. No undisclosed capability observed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:He picked up the lamp and went back across the dark floor, and up through the wing, and out into the night, where the river was loud with barges going down. And on the step outside, with his hand on the cold rail, he let the thought finish at last, the one he had been leaving unfinished for eight days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:"That's a Path." She sounded almost jealous. "That's somebody's architecture, written down from outside, by a person it was being used on. I've been trying to explain that difference to you for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:37:Cael was coming down the lecture range's outside stair when Karis met him at its foot, out of breath, holding two folded slips. The wing's runner had gone looking for him in the library and found her instead, and had given her both, because the runner was a first-year and Karis looked like a person who would know where anybody was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:Lira had her bad leg along the settle, and was rolling a wooden pin up and down the outside of her thigh with both hands, slowly, wincing at the same place on every pass. Brom sat in the big chair with his knees apart and his elbows on them, holding a mug he had not drunk from. Karis sat on the floor with her back to the cold grate and the grey notebook on her knees. She had read the whole of the morning out to them from it, the deltas and the name and both of Withrow's speeches and Gault's two sentences, in order, without a word added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:9:A clean sheet. The heading, *Exception report: floor incident*, because any afternoon outside the routine went up under that heading, whatever it was found to contain. The date. His brief's number. Then, before he wrote a word of the body, he did what he always did before a report that mattered, and called up the auditor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:229:"A minute and a half," said Cael. "The same as the first day. Near enough every time. Both her bad openings fall inside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:233:"Four bouts since you sent it. Four pages. A minute and a half, and then the doors start closing." He looked at her. "Why?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:He had watched Fiske, Force Path, Copper Rank Eight on a certificate two years old, fight four times since Lira's decision, as Lira had wanted him to. Four pages. On every one of them the same thing: a minute and a half of collecting, two bad openings, and then the floor closing behind her opponents like doors in a long corridor, one after another, until there was nowhere left to stand. Fiske never struck anybody. She put her force into the boards where they were about to be, and they arrived on it, and stumbled, and found her hand on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:57:It was a numbered list of every sheet in the file. Beside each number stood one line, never more, saying what that sheet proved and nothing else. There were no adjectives anywhere in it, and nothing that argued. Cael read the first page of it over the counter, upside down. With it, a delegation counsel with a sore back and no patience could find the sheet that carried the whole matter inside a minute and a half. She would not have to ask anybody on the bluff for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:"She asked me last night how long Fiske collects." Cael kept his eyes on the boards. "I told her a minute and a half. So she gave her two minutes. She gave her everything she wanted to know, and every bit of it was true. That's her sequence. That's what she does when she's left alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:225:"Leave it," said Lira. "It's a good question. Fiske asked it too. For the whole minute and a half." She shifted the wet cloth on her hip and looked at Brom straining at the post, his heel lifting and being forced down. "She just didn't get the answer till it was too late to use. Neither did you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:49:Cael sat in his usual place on the east tier, a third of the way up, with Brom on his left and Karis on his right, and did not count the crowd. He had made himself that promise at breakfast. In a yard this size the counting would take all of him, and the hold would go, and he had a hold to keep. So he gave himself the boards and nothing else, and let everything outside the chalk look after itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:Fiske had seen it coming, of course; she had seen everything coming all season. But seeing a thing coming and having somewhere to put a slick floor in front of it are not the same. Lira had come in before there was any floor to close. Fiske turned the half-built declaration into a plain short delivery instead, and it caught Lira on the outside of the left arm, hard, and Lira's right hand was already flat on Fiske's ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:43:Outside, through the high window, he could see a slice of the gate and the end of the training hall. The man on the bench by the training hall door was there, as he was there every afternoon, with his hands on his knees and his hat on the bench beside him. He was one of five, and like the other four he did not trouble to hide; nobody had hidden since the ruling, and somewhere around the second week of the recess Cael had stopped flinching at it, which he counted as progress.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:49:"I'll say the word once," she said. "Here. Then nobody says it outside the four of us. Not in the yard, not in a letter home, not to Quenna, not to Naveth. Not even to Prynn, though Prynn knows already, because Prynn knows everything that happens in her room before it happens."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:There was the gap. He had come on it in the first pass, somewhere on the coast road with a gull's cry outside the shutter. The next three passes had not closed it by a hair. It was the most interesting gap that had crossed his table in a long while. He meant that in the plainest way a craftsman can mean it, the way a joiner is interested in a joint that ought to have failed and has not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:237:"I built it, and I have to wait outside like a parcel." She opened her eyes. "That's the whole point of it, of course. I understand that. I'd just like it noted that I mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:15:"You broke the drop-frame," she said, sitting down opposite him with her bowl. "A Current girl in the third quadrangle has it that you laid a hand on the rail and the rail snapped." She took a spoonful. "And you refused to demonstrate at all, and sat in a chair for an hour with your arms folded, and Gault passed you anyway because he was afraid of the Compact. That's from two Iron boys outside the north hall, and they didn't agree with each other about why he was afraid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:59:He came to a halt just outside arm's reach, his hands at his sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:209:He read it as water reads a hillside. Where would people go, and where would they stop, and from where could they be seen stopping? Three halls on the north edge, each with two sets of doors: six streams that ran together for a quarter of an hour at every change of bell and then broke apart again. The assessment wing sat behind a low wall of its own: one gate, then two doors into the building. Excellent, if you worked inside. Very poor for anyone who wanted to stand outside and watch the gate. The lecture range, four floors high, a stair at either end and every landing open along its whole length, so that a man on one landing could be seen from the far stair by anybody who happened to be looking. The quadrangles built round courts, so many windows facing in that nobody ever looked out of any of them on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:249:*Before I tell them, the want, in my own hand, so it can't come back later in a hat: I want that room. I want it more than I've wanted anything on this bluff since the gate list. My feet went looking for the place a man by the door would see last, in hall three, in the middle of a set, without asking me. That is what wanting it looks like from the outside, and I would rather the three of them heard it from me than worked it out from where I stand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:It came to him late and thin, like a voice through a wall: a pressure gathering high on the outside of his left arm, and then the whole weight of it arriving there at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:There was no sound of anybody moving. There was light. At the head of the upper flight, on the little square landing outside the archive, the man with the bag had stopped at the door and opened the shutter of a dark-lantern a finger's width, because he needed to see the lock. A thin bright wedge of yellow fell down the upper flight and stopped two steps above Cael's corner. It lit the archive door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:147:The Rank Four came out hard, as Cael had seen half the Copper board come out against her in the register's history, gathering his force early and throwing it at her to have it done before she settled. For the first minute she gave ground, and not gracefully. She stepped back badly twice, once nearly off her chalk, and a murmur went along the rim; Cael saw the Rank Four's shoulders lift, a man who has just begun to believe something. Then, at about the minute and a half, Fiske stopped giving ground. She did not seem to do anything in particular. She set herself a little off his line, and when he gathered and came, the force he met was not in front of him at all but beside his leading foot, a short flat push into the floor where his foot was about to land. He landed on it, and it was like stepping onto a stair somebody had moved. He stumbled half a pace, and she was already there with her hand on his collarbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:153:*Price: she has to see first. For the first minute and a half she's collecting, and she opens badly, twice today, because she hadn't finished reading him. Somebody she's never seen could take her early.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:175:"A minute and a half," said Cael. "Near enough. Both her bad openings fell inside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:21:So Cael told him, there on the landing, with his voice low and his back to the wall, because it was easier to say aloud to Brom than to write down. The hold ran all the time; that was its rent, and the rent he could pay, because he had been paying it for a month. But Brom's fragment, the Iron-adjacent read, had two parts, and only one of them was free. The outside of it simply ran, the coarse cool sense on his forearms of something heavy coming, from where. The inside of it was the reach, when he went down into one forearm in particular and felt its line a fraction before it landed. That was what made their drill worth doing, and since the night on the stair the reach and the hold had drawn on one purse. It was a small purse, and it had a bottom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:67:He slept the nine hours and woke a little before the bell with his head clear and the rent behind his eyes no bigger than a coin. He ate at the second bell, early, at a time when nobody would remark on it, and went to his lecture and sat in his usual place. On the way he passed the wing, where the door onto the covered walk was shut and the young clerk stood outside it with two black boxes in his arms, waiting to be let in. Beside him, a little apart, stood the instrument woman with her oil can and her brass rag, looking at the shut door of her own document store with the patient face of somebody who has been told to come back later and fully intends to. It was not her posted day. She had come anyway, to make sure, and Cael liked her for it more than he could have explained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:99:He let it go on purpose and all at once, before the purse could make the choice for him. The next time Brom's forearm came in, Cael did not go down into it but let the outside of the read tell him, coarsely, *heavy, from the right, about there*, which was all it ever told him. Then he met it on the frame alone, turning on the slant he had drilled ten thousand times. He trusted the oak to take what he sent it, and he counted Brom's four in his head instead of feeling it on his skin. The turn came out a hair late and a hair rough, but it was still a turn, and the floor still took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:161:She went back up the stair. Four questions, and she did not need a fifth. She had asked nothing about the boy, because the boy had not been her question; the boy's record was, and the boy's record was this woman's hand. The last answer was the one she would write down tonight, in her own book, under the date. *Seven crosses in a term. Two for sneezing.* A keeper with a mark for *I did not see* was a keeper you could build on, and they were rarer than anyone outside her work would believe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:135:Cael was on the step outside the near door with his back to the jamb and his left arm across his knees. He had got as far as moving his fingers. He was trying to judge whether he could stand, and, without quite meaning to, assembling a reason to be somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:*And I'll watch the coats. Two at the ferry, two at the road's foot, every change of every four hours. They're the one instrument I've got that reads his people from outside. If the coats don't change, nothing came down the road. Any change, four days should show it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:"The Arbiter took about a minute and a half." Her eyes were on the wall's dark bulk, not on him. "It came, and I saw it, and it looked at me, and then the station clerk wrote *Copper* on a sheet and *Rank One*, and that was me. A minute and a half. Two years now I've been trying to get somebody to look longer than that." She was quiet a moment. "That man tomorrow has a room built for nothing but looking. A dozen instruments. He's got a clerk to time your blinks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:135:"An Arbiter station. One day. When the queue moves." She shrugged. "That's not the same. Same minute and a half, nicer manners."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:35:That was all it took to decide what a person was for the rest of her life. She had been fourteen and had not cried until she was outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:37:Today a great many people were going to look at her for a good deal longer than a minute and a half. One of them was the best looker the Compact owned, and he was not here for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:149:The card said that officers of the delegation would not converse with any party under review outside a sitting, and would not acknowledge such a party beyond ordinary courtesy. Havel had read it four times on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:Brom sat for a moment longer. Then he leaned forward and began patting his coat, which lay on the bench under two other people's, and went through every pocket, and finally found what he wanted in a tear in the lining: a pencil worn down to the length of a thumbnail. He unwound the wrap on his right wrist, the right one, which had nothing on it, not the left with Rooke's long sentence inked on the inside where only he would ever feel it. On the outside of the cloth, where anybody passing could have read it, he wrote four words, pressing so hard Cael could see the pencil bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:203:This was nothing like that. This was four words in pencil, on the outside, written on the spot by a man with his head still ringing who did not trust himself to remember them as far as the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:161:*Lira's price: if he touches my paper wrong. This isn't his error. If he touches it at all, it'll be to put it right. I think that's outside her terms. I'll tell her either way.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:197:"I know what he did." She looked out over the empty floor. "It's outside my terms. I said wrong. You were right to keep it." She was quiet again. Then, much lower: "It's worse, though, isn't it? If he'd moved your hall to make you miss an hour, I'd know what he was. I'd be at Withrow's door by noon. Instead he's put it right. On time. Twice initialled." She shook her head slowly. "I don't know what to do about a man who does his work properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:23:The light moved a hand's width across the boards. Somewhere outside a porter was rolling a barrel over cobbles, a long hollow grumble that rose and fell and stopped. The cold came up off the floor into his feet. He sat with his hands loose between his knees and thought about nothing in particular, on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:35:*And a note on myself, because I can see the shape of it now and I'd rather have it in ink. I collect open offers. Reydan's find-me-later, outside a ring two years ago. Vell's session. Edran's promise of a third bout, which Brom borrowed for Lira on the oak after the final; Edran has no notion how far his line has gone. Prynn's shelf, with the gap in it that'll still be there when I'm forty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:5:He had walked past it most days for a month, on the path behind the assessment wing that ran down to the low hall, and every time he had looked at it and every time he had left it alone. It hung beside the door on a square iron nail at the height of his shoulder, a plain black key on a loop of leather gone shiny with handling, and the door it belonged to was locked from the outside. He had written both facts down on his first morning on the bluff, and underlined them, and had not been able to think of a single reason for either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:9:On the thirty-third morning he asked the porter, who was sitting on an upturned pail outside the wash-house cleaning the Copper Crown's case-key with a rag and some ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:15:"Lattice." The porter breathed on the key and rubbed. "There's one instructor teaches it on this hill, and nine students learn it, and that's her hall and nobody else's. Thirty-one years she's had it. She had the lock put on the outside her first year." He held the key up to the light and squinted at it. "You want to go in, you take the key down off the nail. You knock. You wait. If she wants you, she opens it from the inside with her own key, and you hang hers back up. If she doesn't want you, she doesn't open it, and you hang hers back up anyway and go away."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:17:"Why the key outside, then? If she opens it from in?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:139:Outside, the noon light was very bright after the striped dimness of the low hall. He hung the key on its nail and stood with his hand still on the leather loop, looking at it. A month he had walked past it, and a month it had been hanging there, at the height of his shoulder, offering him the plainest piece of information on the whole bluff, and he had left it alone because he could not read the invitation. The invitation had been perfectly simple. *Stop. Knock. Wait.* Caution had hidden it from him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:175:He lay in the dark and went back, as he had not been able to stop himself going back, through every time in two years the read had been quiet and he had taken the quiet as clear. The back stair at the boarding house in Ardenmere, which he had climbed a hundred nights without a lamp because his forearms told him it was empty. The corridor outside the archive at Greyvane, the evening the Compact's officer had walked its length with his cases. The yard behind the residence wing, in the dark before the first bell, where he had turned and turned on frozen stone with his back to the gate. He had trusted all three silences, and every one of them, as it happened, had been empty. But he had not known it. He had only felt nothing, and called the nothing knowledge, and gone on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:19:It had started on the third day with a second-year in the corridor outside the north hall who had stopped talking as Cael passed and then started again a little louder, and it had gone on from there, because people here did not wonder what he was, the way they had at Greyvane; they told each other. Each of them had a version, and each version was finished, and no two of them were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:37:The delegation's card was in Havel's inside pocket, beside the notebook. It was a small stiff card, printed, and every officer on the manifest had been handed one at the coast. Officers of the delegation did not converse with any party under review outside a sitting. They did not acknowledge any such party beyond ordinary courtesy. They did not carry messages, take papers, or accept anything offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:123:The Force girl drove chest-high, and the Stone boy rooted and took it, and she slid off him and came back low, and touched him on the outside of the knee with two fingers as his weight was still going down, so lightly that it was very nearly not a touch at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:59:He had never known how to describe the read to anybody who did not have it. The Wind was a thing he did. The read was more like a thing that happened to the weather on his skin. A weight on the move pressed on the air, and the air pressed on him, a little before the weight itself could, in a thin band along the outsides of his arms and up behind his ears. It told him how heavy and which way. It had never told him why, or what anybody hoped would happen next. It was cheap and silent and gave nothing away to anybody watching, and it would not reach one inch past a pace and a half however he strained it. One month, long ago, had gone to straining it further. Every month after, he had left it to be itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:And a fortnight ago, in a room with a key outside its door, it had told him a lie by saying nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:The passage was full, as it always was at the change of bell, and Cael went along it slowly, because his hip had sent its bill and his right arm would not swing. Heads turned as he went by. Two second-years stopped talking outside the records room. A girl with an armful of practice smocks flattened herself against the wall to let him pass and stared at his arm. Somewhere behind him a man carrying a stack of forms stepped out of the stream, as clerks do, to let the passage go past; Cael did not look at him, because there was nothing in a clerk with forms to look at, and because by then his whole attention had turned inward, to the conversation at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:233:He folded it, and wrote Hesk's name and Fen Street on the outside, and laid it on the table for the first coach, and laid the pen back in its pine case, and closed the lid. Along the passage, through the thin wall, he heard Brom say something, and Karis answer, and Lira laugh at both of them. Then three doors closed, one after another, as people went to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:The instrument woman said so. A woman with an oil can, who came on the posted day of every month and initialled a yellowed card by Gault's door that nobody had ever told her to keep. And this morning she had stood outside her own shut store, not on her posted day, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:131:In five days the porter would chalk two lines on that oak, forty feet apart, and Lira would walk out to one of them on a hip that would be ten days old by then, and Brom would walk out to the other with Rooke's words in pencil on the outside of his wrap. Karis would be on the east tier with the tied batons in her lap. And Cael would be in his usual place, a third of the way up, knowing more about what was happening on those boards than anybody else in the yard, and holding his own edges the whole time, in front of the eleven, without anyone knowing that either thing was happening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:An application of the second class was an application to a body outside the house. She knew which application this had to be, on this evening, from this office.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:131:Brom threw from the wall, a straight sitting blow at whatever instant he chose, while Cael stood on the crack in the boards with his back half turned and had to feel it coming and be gone. Nothing in the house came nearer to a weight falling down a rail. The first run was clean. Cael felt it gather along the outsides of his arms a pace and a half out, turned his shoulder out of its road, and was clear, and Karis timed the gap on the wash-house glass she had borrowed and wrote it down and put her question and got her answer. The second run was clean as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:181:She ran them as a panel would. After every trial she turned half away with Brom and put her head close to his over the sheet and talked low for a minute and a half. Cael stood on the crack in the floor and waited, as he would wait a dozen times tomorrow, and perhaps more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:There had been a hall at Fenmark with a long table and a woman behind it who had spent about a minute and a half on her, and at the end of the minute and a half had written a word and a number: Copper, Rank One. That was three years ago. Since then Lira had fought on a foundry floor and on the Ironyard's planks and in a formal yard at Greyvane, and in front of eight hundred people here, and she had beaten fighters whose paper said Iron. She had a flag on her own file, put there by somebody who had once looked at her properly, and the flag had gone into a queue, and the queue was where it still was. And every morning at the fifth bell the clerk chalked the same word beside her name as he had chalked the morning she arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:203:He found her on the north stair, three steps from the bottom, sitting. Her left leg was out straight along the stone and her hand was pressed flat to the outside of the thigh, hard, and her face had gone white round the mouth. She had walked forty yards in front of the yard without a hitch in her step, and the moment the arch hid her she had stopped being able to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:233:Karis had brought a basin of cold water and a cloth from the wash-house and set them on the common room table with the air of a woman who would not be argued with, and Lira sat with her leg along the settle and the wet cloth folded over the outside of her hip, and let it be done to her, which told Cael more about the hip than any amount of limping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:He walked the north edge first, where the three long halls stood with their high windows facing the morning. The largest had six floors marked out on its boards, and somebody was already on one of them at that hour, alone, running a drill he did not know. The second hall was smaller and its floors were timber, not stone, and when he stood in its doorway and shifted his weight he felt the boards answer him very slightly, a give and a return, as though the floor had a breath of its own. He wrote that down and underlined it and did not yet know why. The third hall was low and set apart behind the assessment wing. Its door was locked from the outside, with the key on a nail beside it, which was a thing he had never seen before and could not think of a reason for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:47:It was in his own hand, from the evening before, in the middle of a page of others like it: *Current Path, tall, dark braid, third year by the tag. Favours the right leg coming down stairs. Old injury, healed short.* It was a good entry, and he remembered making it: the braid, the stair outside the north hall, and the small hitch in the right leg on the second step down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:243:*I'm not on the wall. The Gold fellow isn't either. Two of us outside the count, for opposite reasons.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:55:A second shell had come up on the off arm, which had been carried forward and close in so that it could, and it was already set across the space Cael had moved into. He felt it on the read a breath too late to stop. He turned his shoulder and took the shell on the outside of his forearm, where it burst and stung, and Edran's lead hand came round inside it with the edge of the forearm turned out and laid itself along his ribs. It barely touched, but it was clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:211:"I've been looked at like a number all my life," she said. "A minute and a half, and a word on a sheet. I didn't know there was something worse." She turned to go down. "Being worked round. Like furniture." At the bottom of the stair she looked back up at him. "Count him properly, then. If you're going to pay for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:219:Havel had sat at the foot of the long table on the fifteenth while that man's index was turned over by the best counsel the delegation had, sheet by sheet, for most of a day, and had watched it find every paper she asked for inside a minute and a half. He had never been in a position to tell a registrar that his file was good, and he was not in one now. He could hand back a book with his initials in the right column and his eyes on the right line, and so he did that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:311:He read it through and changed nothing, then blotted it and shut the book, and sat for a while with his hand flat on the cover and the rain going on outside. The ice went to bed with him, and he slept better than he had any right to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:29:Cael went up the back stair of the records hall before breakfast anyway, because he had never yet walked into a room he had not tried to see first. The east room's door was shut. On the landing outside it, on a stool, with a mug in one hand and a ledger on his knee, sat the inky-knuckled clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:23:Brom came down into the sunk floor slowly, with his wraps on, the left with Rooke's long sentence inked on its inside and the right with four words in pencil on its outside. He stood on his chalk with his arms loose. Tarn stood on his, lighter, narrow, his weight a little forward on his toes like a bird that has seen a crumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:91:"Greyvane," she said. "The whitewashed room, with the slate on the nail outside it. You all remember. Brom, you were witness twice and ate an apple through both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:147:"I want this in the minute in plain words. I have never read anything like this about any Path. Not in the founding-era surveys. Not in anything my instructors kept. Not in anything Quenna ever showed me, and she showed me most things in the end. You have written down how a Bronze-tier Shadow Path pays for its declaration, at what rate, on what curve through a day, and three of the places where it can't reach. From outside. In forty days. Off a man who has never once let you see him use it." She took the ledger back. "When I did the same to you, it took me the better part of a year, and I had your permission in writing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:129:At Fenmark she had been fourteen and frightened and nobody had told her anything. She had put her hand where a clerk pointed, and something had come and looked at her for about ninety breaths, and then a man who had not once lifted his head from his page had written *Copper* and *Rank One*, and that had been her. She had not cried until she was outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:189:The brass weight went down. The rain had begun outside after all, very fine, and the window by Karis was misting at its lower edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:Gault leaned toward the Mire instructor and the two of them bent their heads over the sheet and talked low, with the Ash instructor leaning in to listen, and after that the clerk would be sent to the north wall and would take his time over it. A minute and a half, perhaps two, with nothing for anybody on the brass to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:209:It was not a spark, and it was not a letting-go either, though that was what it felt like at the very end; those were both the outside of it. Underneath both lay a choice of an instant. There is an instant when the unburnt turns into the burning, and what he had taken from her let him put that instant wherever he wished at the surface of his own skin. It was a decision point. Everything that came after it was the copper's business, or the water's, or the wood's, and only the moment was his. That was why it would not hold, and why it cost him the same whatever size he asked for, and why each one had to be made new: you could not hold a decision, you could only make another one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:51:By then there were a great many letters, because Bracken did not wait for one answer before he wrote the next: he wrote by every coach, sometimes two letters in one packet, numbered on the outside so that Karis would read them in the order he had written them and not the order the post delivered them. Karis kept them in a bundle tied with grey string, which she had cut from the end of one of the delegation's boxes when they came back at the end of the winter term and had been saving for something worth tying. The bundle was as thick as two fingers now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:3:The stair outside the lecture range had fourteen steps, a landing, and fourteen more, and at the change from the second bell to the third about ninety people went down it in the time it takes to boil an egg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:13:So he settled on a tally, about as rough a tool as a pencil could make. It was a baseline of a sort, the first he had ever taken of anything outside his own body. But a rough tool used the same way every time will still draw you a line, and this one had begun drawing. In his second week it stood at well over a third. By his sixth it had slipped a little under. Down the inside margin there was a column of fractions now, each with its date, falling the way a fever falls, slowly and with bad days in it. Another month and he would have a curve. With a curve he could say how long a name took to cool on a hill like this, which seemed worth knowing, since he expected to be carrying his for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:157:Take the east hall at the second bell. The gallery's stair came down at the east end and its rail screened the floor nearest it, so a watcher wanted the far end, the west, just past the post where the rail turned, where the whole floor and both doors lay open below. The Crown yard on a practice afternoon wanted the high west tiers, where the eye ran down the length of the rings and not across them, with the north stair and the wing's gate at the edge of sight. The stair outside the lecture range wanted the second landing, on the wall side, where the crowd going up and down made a moving screen between the watcher and anybody who might think to look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:*Iron Skin. Copper.* Four words in pencil on the outside of a wrap, by a man who had been hit too hard to trust his own memory as far as the gate, and had won anyway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:At the foot of the road, where it left the river street, two men in grey coats were sitting on a low wall outside a chandler's. They were not the two from the bridge. They did not so much as turn their heads as the four of them came onto the hill, and Cael added them to the count and did not look back.

