editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-05.md:235:Five carts were queued at the district gate when he reached it, and the guard took a little under a minute over each. Cael timed the third and fourth without meaning to, by counting under his breath. The gate itself was a low stone arch where the Outer wall ran out into open country, and the guard post beside it was about the size of a garden shed. It mostly existed so that someone could look at merchant manifests, and the man in it looked at them with the speed of someone who had done it nine thousand times and expected nine thousand more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:261:"*Neither number is us.* Nineteen years, and I've never once said that sentence out loud." She looked at the stone. "I've thought it since the garden court. I never had anybody to say it to who'd have known what I meant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:119:It was the garden.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:121:There had been a pear tree, and a book with a red spine. It was a history of the coastal leagues, and she had taken it off the shelf of the feeder school's library without quite asking. She was thirteen. She was reading it on the bench under the pear tree on the south side of the garden court, in the empty hour after the midday meal, because the court was nearly always empty then, and she was nearly always alone in it. She was already a year ahead of everybody she was taught with, and taller than most of them, and she had found that a bench nobody else wanted was the most comfortable place in the school.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:125:Not the birds; the birds had been quiet already. The court. The gravel and the box hedges and the pear leaves went still in a way she had never seen anything go still, as though the whole garden had stopped to listen for something. Overhead the sky was exactly as it had been. It had changed in the court. It lay on the paths differently. It lay on her hands on the book differently.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:145:She had been Auremont's since twelve, a year before the garden. The house had seen something in a set of entrance papers and sent for her, and kept her near, and waited for the rest. Afterwards nothing in her life had been left to happen by itself. Floors were relaid to her measure. An evaluator who wanted a look at her had his station opened before the day began, and came, and looked. Whole classes were assembled round her and broken up again when she grew past them. Other people spoke of climbing; she had never once had to look for the next rung. It was always there, put down where her foot would go, by people who were very good at their work. At Auremont the day of the garden court was a half-holiday now, with a demonstration in the afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:3:Corbin came over the rope like a man stepping over a low wall in his own garden.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-40.md:183:"Five," said Corbin, and nodded, as though the number confirmed something he had always suspected about the world. Then he turned and walked off toward the water bucket, square and unhurried, a man stepping over a low wall in his own garden. On his way he passed Dessa's bench, and Dessa said something to him that Cael could not hear, and Corbin laughed again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:63:He had been once, a long time ago, in two borrowed farm carts with a wheel lost in a ford, and had come fourth in the Copper on the figures, and every one of them knew the story because he had told it at the confluence over a cup of something he was not used to. Since then he had not said another word about the city. On the fifth evening, when the others had gone in, Cael found him sitting on the bench outside the waystation's kitchen door with the case on his knees and his hands folded on its lid, looking at the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:273:The first three days were farm country, rolling and wet, with sheep in the hollows and woodsmoke lying flat along the valleys in the mornings. On the fourth day the hedges gave out and the walls began, grey drystone walls that ran up the hillsides and over the tops, ruler-straight. On the fifth the farms got smaller and further apart and the inns got colder, and on the sixth morning there was ice in the horse trough at the waystation for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:"I said at the wall I'd tell you the rest of the estate wall," he said. "The yard man. The month of sitting." He turned the hand over. "I thought he'd put me there to be out of the way. While they talked about me in the long room. I hated him for it. Every morning for a month, after the station, he sat me down on the flags with my back to the wall of the kitchen garden and said *stay there till I call you*, and went in, and left me. No post. No falls. Nothing. Just the wall, and the cold, and the gardeners going by with their barrows. I was fourteen and I'd just been read Copper and everybody in the house had gone quiet, and I sat there with nothing to do but be angry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:63:It was the same hand: a boy's hand on a shaft and a man's hand on a beam and a boy of fourteen with his back against a garden wall. None of them were reaching; they were all only resting there, flat, on something that carried weight, and letting the weight come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:161:He did not know whether he could do that. He did not know anybody who had ever asked it of him before, except a man in a lit alcove with a garden wall in his past, and an old man at home with his hand on a mill shaft. He found he was smiling, alone, on the cold stone, with his head aching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:17:Then the house had set out for the finals in two farm carts lent by the chancellor's brother. On the fourth day the second cart lost a wheel in the middle of a ford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:203:He had watched grammar change before, in his time. It was never announced. A word that had lived for years in one kind of paper would begin to turn up in another kind, a little out of place, like a farm cart in a city street. Nobody remarked on it the first time, or the second. By the tenth time it had stopped looking out of place, and the people who used it had forgotten that it had ever been anywhere else. He had once seen *resettlement* travel in that way from a report on a flooded valley to a report on a quarrelsome town, in less than a year, and nobody who used it in the second report had ever read the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:87:"There's a way they survey a hill farm for the tithe. They don't count every sheep. Nobody could. They count the folds. They take one fold in every so many and count every animal in it properly, and they write down which fold and which day. For the rest they write how many folds, and roughly how big, and leave it." She turned her pen in her fingers. "The tithe man isn't lying about the sheep he didn't count. He's writing down that he didn't count them, and how he guessed. It's the same number of sheep either way. It's a different kind of honest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:3:The dog came out of a farm gateway in the second hour, as though it had been waiting there for him by arrangement. It was thin and brown, with a white bib on its chest and one ear torn at the tip, and it fell in at his left side without looking up, matched its trot to his walk, and asked for nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-01-the-shattered/manuscript/chapter-06.md:95:It came quickly: a high-sided farm cart heaped with turnips, pulled by one big dun mare going downhill at a trot that was not quite under control. Its wheels were rimmed with iron, but the road was soft dust here, and they made almost no sound at all. The driver was slumped on the bench with the reins slack in his lap and his hat over his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-15.md:231:"I know. That's why I'd like it not to be us." Lira tossed the core over the wall into the kitchen garden. "Did you see her let him take that shoulder? In the third. She could have slipped it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-24.md:177:She ate barley at the midday meal, which she hated, and a heel of the kitchen's bread, which she also hated, and an apple she took off a second-year's tray without asking, which she did not hate at all. Brom sat across from her and talked. He talked about Wray's lecture on how a hardening Path carries load through the heel, and then about the honey thief, who had been seen near the kitchen garden with a spoon and a guilty face, and then, at great length and with real feeling, about barley, and why it was the most misunderstood grain in Valdris, and how the kitchen at Velmere had served it with cream to make it pretend to be something else, which in his opinion was the beginning of most of what had gone wrong with Velmere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:The notices were ordinary: a change to the lamp oil allowance in the residence wing, a lecture on Current Path footwork moved from the third bell to the second, a reminder that the kitchen garden was not a shortcut. The instructor read each one in the same patient voice, and Cael listened to every word as if it mattered, because the alternative was to feel the backs of two hundred heads deciding not to turn around.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:11:Lira was three rows ahead, and she did not turn either. But halfway through the notice about the garden she lifted one hand, as if to scratch her neck, held up two fingers where only he could see them, and put the hand down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-37.md:147:"Six." Keth crouched in front of him, with the blade across his own knees, so that his face was lower than the boy's. "Six is nothing. Do you know what I had, my second year? Eleven. Eleven in a row, on the side floors, to people who've all gone home to farm since." He said it seriously. "Dace wanted to take me off his wall. I told him it was an educational period. I'd been sent to school by everybody in the building, I said, and I was learning a great deal, and I'd thank him not to interrupt my studies."

