editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-59.md:95:"Don't." She put her spectacles back on. "You earned every line of it. All I did was hold the pen." She reached down beside her chair and brought up a second sheaf, much thinner, perhaps six leaves, already tied. "And that's your friend's. Iron Skin. He came and asked me this afternoon for his line, for the gate, and stood at the end of my table with his hat in his hands like a boy sent to the master." She set it on top of Cael's. "That one's only his results, stamped, which any keeper will do for any fighter going to an academy that asks. I've done it eleven times in thirty years. His is the twelfth. Yours is the first of the other kind." She looked at him over the spectacles. "Don't tell him that. He'll think I like you better."
