editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md-133-Cael saw it from the first contact, because he knew what to look for. All five of Marek's stakes were still there. Brom had not closed one of them. The half-beat after his drive was still open. The quarter-beat on his pivot was still open. The board under his back heel still gave. But every one of them now had a price on the road in, and the price was paid in Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:135:Marek went to the half-beat after the drive. He found it as he had found it before, and scored, and the flag went up. And as he came out of it, Brom's other forearm was already waiting at the one angle the exit allowed, loaded with everything an Iron Skin could put behind a forearm, and Marek came out of his touch into it. The return was heavier than the touch. Much heavier. The flag went up for Halcenvane.
