editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md-71-She was fast. That was the first thing, and it was wrong in a way the floor could feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:73:Iron Skin fighters did not need to be fast. Their Path was a hardening, a skin that took blows and gave nothing back but the iron under it, and most of them stood and let the other fighter come and break on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md-75-She did not stand. She came off her chalk in three quick steps and struck before Brom had finished setting his feet: a hardened forearm across his ribs, short and hard, the blow of somebody who has hit grown men at fairs for money and never wasted a swing. It rang. Brom had not hardened in time.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md-125-Brom hardened before the hook.
