editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-15-The old man wrote it, slowly and well, and gave him the key.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:He took down everything in the press that so much as mentioned Iron Skin, which was three books. There was a big brown register of the Paths of the realm, a hundred years old and re-bound twice, and a smaller book on the defensive Paths in particular, and a guild almanac from a city up the coast with a chapter on the families who trained them. He carried all three to the table nearest the stove and sat with them through the whole of the cold afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-19-The register was laid out as the Compact laid out everything, in rows. He had read its front pages before, in the spring, when he was trying to understand the ladder he had been put at the bottom of. There was the table of the metals, Copper and Iron and Bronze and Silver and Gold, with ten ranks to each and a line under the tenth where the station had to look at you again. There was the list of what each metal let you do and where it let you go: Copper the outer districts and the limited market; Iron the whole city, and the guild district with your credentials in your hand; Bronze the administrative core as well, where the gates were. And after the front pages came the Paths themselves, each one a row, with its branch and the metals at which anybody had ever been recorded holding it, and a line or two of what it did. There was no row in it for anybody who had not been given a Path, and he had stopped looking for one a long time ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:21:The register gave Iron Skin a line among the defensive Paths, between a Path for turning blows and another for standing, and said it hardened the body against force. It had been recorded at every metal from Copper to Gold, and at none above. The book on defensive Paths gave it a little more, and all of it was the same thing said at greater length. The almanac gave it four pages.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-23-He read the four pages twice, because he did not at first believe them. They were about who owned it. Here were the three guild houses that taught it, and when each had been founded and by whom. Here was which of them had quarrelled with which, and which families had sent their children to which house for four generations, and what those children had become. One page was nothing but a list of marriages. And in the middle of the second page, between the founding of one house and the founding of the next, there was a single sentence on what the Path did. *Its practitioners raise a hardness of the surface of the body at need, the art of which is the property of the houses named above.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-39-Corrin found him rather than the other way round. He was a big grey Stone fighter with a face like a split log, who had been fighting in the Ironyard since before Dace kept the slate. He no longer fought much, but he sat on the end of the front bench most nights with his hands on his stick and told whoever sat next to him what was wrong with the bout in front of them. He stopped Cael at the water barrel on the second evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:"You're asking round about Iron Skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-43-"I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:"Because of the big one." Corrin did not wait for an answer. "I fought one once. Years back, in a port up the coast, before your mother had you. A woman, not big. Iron Skin, Iron tier, and she looked at me like I was a horse she was thinking of buying." He worked his right hand open and shut on the head of his stick. The knuckles were thick, and two of them did not close all the way. "I'll tell you what it's like and you can make of it what you want. It's not her hitting you. She hardly hit me at all. It's your own hit. You put your best into her, everything you've got, and the hit comes back up your arm the wrong way, on a line you didn't send it on. Your wrist knows before your eyes do. Like punching water that decided, halfway, to be a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-47-Cael wrote it down while the old man watched him do it.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-63-"Not that I noticed. Why would she? I was doing all the work for her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:65:The second man Cael asked was a Blade from Orvet's gym who had lost to an Iron Skin in the second city, and he wanted mostly to talk about the injustice of it, and about a keeper who had scored it wrong, and about his own left knee. Cael listened politely for as long as he could and wrote down one word, *knee*, which turned out to be about the man and not the Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md-67-Neither of them could tell him how it worked. They could tell him what it was like to be on the wrong end of it, and that was something; but it was a bruise and not a map. How a body learned to do it, what it cost, where it ran out: that lived inside three guild houses up the coast, behind four pages of marriages, in books that would never come down to a dye works in an Unranked District for a copper.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md-5-His semifinal was at the fourth bell on the third floor, against a woman he had fought before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:7:Cael knew her before she had her coat off. She had been a sheet in Rooke's profiles stack in the first week of the year, marked more heavily than the sheets round it: a fair-day fighter from a market house in the hills, Iron Skin like Brom, Copper Rank Four on her certificate, with four bouts on her record and every one of them won and every one of them short. *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.* At the wool town she had walked Brom backward round his own chalk by the arm in front of a muttering gallery, and he had beaten her two touches to one in the fourth exchange, and the figures had come up twenty-three to twenty-two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md-9-She was perhaps eighteen, broad in the shoulders, with her hair cropped close to her skull, and she still had the fair-day fighter's habit of standing loose and easy until the instant she was not. The lean coach in brown was at her rail with his arms folded, exactly as he had stood in the sheep market. Her market house had come up through its own region on her back, Karis said, and had brought no other entry to any bracket in the city. For a week she had been cutting through her side of the Copper draw, and not one broadside had printed a line about her.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md-13-| Cael | Sixteen (turned on the eleventh of Sowing, Book 4; the date is never set beside a season word, OWNER #35). Healthy, rested, and trained: sixty-one floor sessions over the recess. No current injuries: B4 closed him fit (the quarter-burst's hip load ended the night of the twenty-third of Reaping; the plate's right-shoulder ache eased by the week's end). The leading hip carries the Wind fragment's old strain (crest to inner knee after a hard evening), and the landing beat is still unshortened. The right shoulder pays Pressure, "four blows a sitting", the circle's private cap and not a registry schedule (B4 ch62; OWNER #36: the plate/Pressure shoulder is the right). The Shadow fragment's rent runs from waking, spent or not; drifts come in the gaps, never in the work, and the weekly count is falling; the working ledger's day count since the stair and the protocol *Hold. First.* are the discipline. Arbiter dark since the Kindling. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md-14-| Lira | Wind Path, Iron-tier, Rank One, formal: the Ostrand registry station's Arbiter evaluation on the fifth of the new month, fifty-one minutes ("They didn't give me anything today. They caught up."); the porter changed the word and moved her name the next day; Gault filed, Ilsev witnessing. Season record certified "Eleven bouts unbeaten. None lost." The Copper Crown is retired on the shelf over the settle ("There. It's retired."). Fit: the semifinal's hip strain (deep, nothing torn) had a month's knitting left at term's end, and nothing of it carries into this book. She trains at dawn on Rooke's speed line. She meant to write to Fiske over the recess (Fiske: "North tier, next season. I'll be watching the Iron bracket."); whether she did is open to M1–M3. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:15:| Brom | Iron Skin Path, Copper formal ("until somebody filed for him"); Iron-grade in practice. Third in Rooke's cohort; lost the Copper final to Lira by one beat in the fourth exchange, on the mended seam. The force-redirect seam is repaired (Rooke's nineteen exercises, four a week; he stops finishing the absorption and drives while still hard on the struck side). Recess sheet: "Nineteen. Round three. Four a week." Fit; the final's iced knee and strapped right knuckles are B4's and do not carry. [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md-16-| Karis | Karis (Dellenmoor), Ember Path, Iron Rank Three (unchanged all B4). Fit. She spent ten weeks of the recess on the clause argument, in the named top-floor reading room (*Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.*, Bracken's hand, standing through the recess). [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md-17-| Seln | Shadow Path, Bronze. Teaching assistant in the assessment wing (the copying table behind the counter; his one sound, "Mm"); the recess counter sheet names *S. Seln* against every working day of the recess and nobody else. At his desk the whole recess. The quarterly went out sealed on the twenty-sixth of Reaping: *Continuation of embedded coverage recommended.* [B4-reconciled 2026-10-05] |
--
