editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-69-Glass ran on discrete commitments. Cael had known that in outline since his second week, when he crossed out a page of notes about brittleness and started again, but now he watched it close enough to see the grain. A Glass declaration did not grow or bend. When Edran called a shell along his forearm it came up all at once, bright and hard and finished, like ice that had decided to be ice everywhere in the same instant. Once it was there, it could not be changed. He could not thicken it on one side or thin it on another, or turn its edge a little further round as a strike came in. If he wanted a different shape, he had to let the first one go and call a new one, and letting go meant bursting: the shell broke outward in that glittering spray that carried force away with it, and then it was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:71:It was the exact opposite of Lira's Path. Wind could be feathered; Lira could start moving one way and change her mind halfway through the motion, and the wind in her went with her, adjusting as she went. It was the opposite of Brom's, too, because Iron Skin could be laid on late and pressed harder as the blow arrived. Glass did neither. A Glass fighter chose before he moved, committed everything to the choice, and lived with it until it broke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-73-*Choose first,* Cael wrote in the margin, and then stopped and looked at the words, because they were his own rule, the one the Compression fragment had beaten into him on the road and Lira had beaten into him again before the first sitting. Glass was a whole Path built on the lesson he was still learning.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-233-"I know. I want it to wake the way it's used to waking. Quietly. So I can tell it no."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md:235:Brom looked at him, and then set his left palm on Cael's forearm without any further argument. Iron Skin came up under the skin, and he leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-18.md-237-They did ten. Each time Cael waited for the force to settle into the bone and chose the road before the fragment could choose one, out and back along the line it came in, a quarter's width and no wider. He did not let himself imagine the channel any broader than that. Five of the ten caught. Each of the five went home into Brom's wrist with the small solid knock he knew by now. Each one cost exactly what the quarter had always cost, the hot bar across the collarbones and two breaths he could not take, and none of them went into a joint.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-55-In every hall Cael had known, Brom was the problem nobody solved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:57:At the Ironyard, men twice his age had come off the boards shaking their hands out and laughing ruefully, because hitting Brom was like hitting a door that hit back. At Greyvane he had gone through the defensive cohort in a term, and Wray had needed her own two hands to find the one place in him that gave. He was big and he was patient. Iron Skin made the surface of him dense at the instant of contact, so that a strike landed on something more like stone than flesh, and then he did the thing he had built for himself in the years before anybody taught him anything. He let the strike commit, all the way, and only then took it and turned it, so that the force went on past him into nothing, and the striker went with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-59-The quick girl touched him on the shoulder before he had finished setting his feet.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-67-None of them ever explained, and none needed to. Each correction they made to him was written on his body in the one language every fighter on the floor could read, and he read it, and stored it, and was touched again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:69:None of the touches were hard; there was no call for them to be. Iron Skin would have taken a great deal more than any of them carried, and Brom stood under them like a wall under rain. But every one of them was clean, which was the whole point of them, and every one arrived somewhere Brom had not finished arriving at, and on a scored floor each of them would have gone up on the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md-70-
