editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:163:It was not a touch. The table did not call it. A strike taken on a set, hardened guard was not clean; it had arrived exactly where Brom had decided it would. But it had cost him. Cael could see the cost from forty feet away. Iron Skin hardened on a fuel neither of them had ever managed to name, though they had both tried, and the fuel ran out, and it ran out faster under blow after blow with nowhere to send them. The turn had always been the thing that paid the hardening back, by passing the force along. Without the turn every strike stayed where it landed, and Brom had to hold it there himself.
