editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-19-A few of the children laughed. The instructor did not seem to have meant it as a joke, and Cael decided he liked him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:A chart hung on the wall behind the lectern, large and painted on stiff canvas, old enough that its corners had been mended twice. Across the top ran the seven tiers in their colours: Copper in dim orange, Iron in silver-grey, Bronze in warm gold, Silver bright as a coin, Gold a deep amber, Platinum white touched with blue with a small note beside it that said *historical*, and at the far end a seventh box with no colour in it at all, only the word *Void* and a question mark. Down the left side ran the Paths themselves, grouped into families and painted in neat capitals — Movement: Wind, Current, Storm; Hardening: Stone, Iron Skin, Shield, Anchor, Wall; Edge: Blade, Glass — and then Ember and Ash, Force and Pressure, Mire and Tide, and more below those, a long column of names, some of which Cael had only ever seen written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-23-"This chart is copied from the Compact's own schedule, the list the registry uses," the instructor said. "Every Path an Arbiter can assign is on it. The families are ours, for teaching; the list is theirs. If your Arbiter tells you a Path, it will be one of these. If it isn't on this chart, it isn't a Path." He tapped the top row with a pointer. "Tiers first. You all know the colours. Who can tell me how many ranks are in a tier?"
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-207-"I'm going to try very hard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md:209:The twenty students in the room were all of the same family, Stone and Shield and Anchor and Iron Skin, along with one tall boy whose skin went faintly grey and grainy when he was nervous, which was apparently always. Wray stood at the front with a slate board behind her and used no notes. She drew a curve. It started low on the left at a mark labeled *Copper One* and rose steeply through the early ranks and then began to bend, so that by the middle of Copper it was rising more slowly and by the upper ranks it had nearly flattened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-05.md-211-"Form efficiency. How much more you get out of a declaration, rank by rank, by refining the way you use it. Not by getting stronger. By getting better." She tapped the steep part. "Early on, every hour of form work pays. You learn where your weight goes, you stop wasting motion, and your first declaration does twice what it did at Kindling without your gaining an ounce of raw output." She moved the chalk along the bend. "Then it slows. Somewhere around the middle of a tier, for most people, form work stops paying the way it did. You've learned the big lessons, and the small ones are smaller. And here—" She tapped the flat part. "—is what this school, and every school I know of, calls the ceiling: the point past which refining your form gives you so little that you're better off spending the hours on raw output instead. More force. More endurance. Bigger declarations, earned the slow way through rank."
--
