editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-81-He did not say *yes* and he did not say *no*. He ate the rest of his stew, slowly, all of it, and wiped the bowl with a piece of the loaf, and put the bowl down, and then he began to ask questions.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:83:He asked them one at a time, in his flat careful voice, and he waited for each answer before he asked the next. What did Greyvane teach about Iron Skin, if anything? Was there anybody on the staff who read, or who had ever stood in front of somebody who read? How long did a standard student stay? Who paid, and how much, and was there work in the town for a man who had to earn it? Could a Copper with a clean line and no complaints against him simply walk to the gate and ask to be taken, the same as anybody else? What did such a man have to show, and to whom?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-85-Cael answered the ones he could, which were few. Lira answered one or two more, from Fenmark. At Fenmark, she said, a standard student came in at fourteen or fifteen and stayed four years, if he was not sent home; the town round an academy always had work for anybody with a strong back and no pride, carrying and kitchens and the stables; and every academy she had ever heard of kept somebody on its staff whose whole business was reading. "They'd not have a reader like you," she said to Brom. "Nobody has a reader like you. But they'll have somebody who'll know what you are when you stand in front of him."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-95-"Not the name," said Brom. "Anybody can have a name. Whether they want the house. Whether they want a letter from your father, or your father's friend, or the station that did your reading, saying who you are and what you're for." He had his big hands flat on the table, one on each side of the empty bowl, as Lira had held hers. "Because if they do, I'll not go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:"You've got a real classification," said Cael slowly. "Iron Skin. Copper. From an Arbiter. You don't need any provision."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-56.md-99-"I know I don't. That's what frightens me." Brom said it without any heat at all. "The provision's for people the paper can't hold. The paper holds me fine. It holds me in the place my family's letters put me, with a man's note in the margin saying I was taught wrong from the start and ought to be read again at a better station." He turned his head toward the window, where the cold was coming in round the frame. "I got up from that table at fourteen because I wasn't going to be measured by what was written down about me in a room I'd been sent out of. I'll not walk into a new room and hand them the same paper and let them measure me by it all over again. Not even if the paper says something kind."
--
