editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md-109-Gold ran down through the family. His grandmother's card said Gold, and so did his mother's, though if you went by the figures and not the card his mother sat nearer the top of Silver than the bottom of Gold. Nobody in the house had ever said so; the card said Gold, and the card was what people saw. His sister was two years older than he was and already two years into a guild placement in a city by the sea, and she was spoken of at the long table in the voice the family kept for a thing that was going to go well. Nobody in three generations of that house had been read at a station and come out anywhere but at the top of the ladder, or near enough to it that nobody mentioned the gap over the soup.
