editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-175-The notations were as short and dry as they had been in the slate country, and every first exchange in them was a verdict. Every touch was marked against a join, a place where one thing turned into another: the change of a guard from one hand to the other, the turn of a reset, the line of a shoulder as it set. Beside every touch stood the same small mark, *the same join, found and then worked*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:"He'll find every join I've got," said Brom. "That's what he does. So I've been asking myself where my joins are." He pointed, without looking, at his own left side. "That's the one everybody will tell him about. Page three. The old seam. It's mended, and everybody who's bought my file knows it's mended, and they'll tell him anyway, because it's the only thing on page three worth the price."
