editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:189:He worked it in the Iron Skin hall in the mornings with the Stone reserve, turning left a hundred times, two hundred, while the reserve came at the door with an open hand. He worked it on the oak in the afternoons with Rooke, who came at it with a stick. He worked it with Cael in the evenings, because Cael's read could see the door begin to open, and could tell him whether it was open a hair less than yesterday. It was. And then a hair less again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-191-In the fourth week the Stone reserve stopped getting through it at all, and in the sixth Rooke's stick stopped getting through it. In the eighth Cael watched Brom turn left with the read at its sharpest, and the door was hardly there at all: there was only a heavy man changing hands so smoothly that the change happened inside his body, under his skin, where no hand could reach it.
