editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:3:He came the next morning, at the end of the drilling, when Lira had put her staff up on its pegs and Cael was sitting on the alcove bench with the grey book open and his hip telling him about the second burst.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:Cael saw him first in the edge of things, as he saw most things now: a broad shape leaving the slate and coming across the bare stone where the big bouts went, not hurrying and not slowing. Lira saw him a moment later. She had been wringing the sweat out of her wrist cloth, and she stopped with it twisted in both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:"Brom. Iron Skin Path. We fight two weeks from today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:"What do you know about Iron Skin?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:35:He turned to go. Then he stopped half turned, with one shoulder in the alcove and one out of it, and looked down at the grey book on Cael's knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:47:"He didn't say a single word to me. He looked at me twice." She put the wrist cloth on the bench beside Cael, carefully. "I counted him. Not his steps. His words. Everything he said, from *Brom* to the end." Her lips moved. "Ninety-odd. Not one of them was *hello*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:61:He wrote it in the grey book on the bench while Lira went to fetch the bread, and the page held very little, which was the first thing it told him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:63:*Brom.* He wrote the name at the head. Under it, *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known. Iron-equivalent in Vell's book* (Dace would not clear the main floor for less, and Dace had chalked it at the top of the slate in yellow before the sweepers were done). Under that went the whole of what he knew about Iron Skin. It came to four lines, and he did not need to look anything up for any of them. It was a Path, filed with the defensive ones in every list he had ever seen. It made the body hard, or parts of it. Whoever trained it kept the rest to themselves, and nobody outside had any reason to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:He looked hard at the second sentence. Every honest sum he could run said it. He did not know Iron Skin. He did not know this man. He had been watched by him for three weeks and more, from close, while he had watched nothing back. The calm arithmetic he ran on every bout before he agreed to it came out, this time, on the other side of the line, and he had never once made a bout go better by lying to the page about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:107:They were short, even for Vell. Each one had the man's name, his Path, the exchange in which it ended. Under every one of the seven, in the narrow upright hand, there was the same thing in slightly different words, as if she had tried each time to find a new way to say it and each time had come back to the old one. *Took everything; gave nothing first.* *Did not begin.* *Opponent's own blows.* *Stood.* Under the fifth win, against an Iron-equivalent Force from the tannery end, she had written only *Stood*, and then, a little apart from it, in a smaller hand: *Force retired with the right wrist.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:137:Cael went back to the bench and wrote it under Brom's name, under *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:179:He filled himself until his ribs hurt. He went short, and locked, and went short again off the edge of the lock, and locked again, and in the second lock he felt the breath go out of the bottom of him. He knew before the lock let go that there would be no third. He asked for it anyway, because he had said he would. Something in the hip moved, and stopped, and he came out of the second lock onto his knees with the alcove going grey at the edges, the lamps turning to smudges, and a ringing in his ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:191:*WIND-ADJACENT. Conditions (add): chain of two. Fill first, past need; spend half on the first burst; hold the rest through the lock; spend it as the lock lets go. Six in seven tonight. Claim: repeatable. Evidence: seen, L. counting. Ruling: yes. Costs (add): the fill shows. Chest up before I go. A new tell, and a big one. Chain of three: not there. Asked once to know. Grey, ringing, knees. Ruling: no. Don't ask again to find out what I already know.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:He had been that boy's contact of record for most of a year. He had sat across a pie from him once, at a table in the district, and been asked whether he knew what a flag was, and had told the truth, which was that he did not. Three days after a bout in a yard, he had opened this file at his own desk and found a grey slip pinned inside the cover that nobody in his section had put there: a printed head, one date, and a code with a prefix he had never seen. He had locked the file in his drawer that afternoon and written nothing about it, and told nobody, then or since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:213:He wondered, for no reason he could have given, what the boy was doing now, this grey Thursday afternoon. Sitting on a bench somewhere with a book open on his knee, most likely, watching somebody who did not know yet how much was being written down about him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:225:The grey slip was gone from inside the cover. Working slips came off when a file went down to be shelved; that was the registry's rule, and the clerks kept them or burned them by some other rule nobody had ever told him. He turned to the classification block on the first leaf, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:The designation had no prefix he knew. Its shape was the shape he remembered from the slip, the run of letters and the gap and the figure after it, and he was nearly sure it was the same code. He could not be sure. On the afternoon he found the slip he had sat with his working log open in front of him, eleven years of plain black books bought with his own money and every file he had ever opened written up in them in his own hand, and he had left that day's page empty on purpose, because a thing written down could be found. So the one thing he needed to check this line against was nowhere: not in the registry, not in his log, only in his memory of a slip of grey paper that had come off its pins and gone wherever the clerks sent such things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:She stood behind the bench with her arms folded on the top of the alcove wall and her chin on her arms, and said nothing for most of the hour. Brom had the newcomers again, the boy with the tooth and the girl and the tall lad, and two more from Keth's ring whom Cael had not seen before. He gave each of them a copper and let them hit him, and sent them sideways, and paid the next. Cael counted on his wrist and wrote in the middle column. Behind him Lira breathed slowly through her nose, as she did when she was reading somebody, and once, when the tall lad's kick came back past him off the edge of Brom's foot, she made a small sound in her throat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:55:"I hate that you keep everything I say," she said. "I hate it. I'm going to start saying stupid things on purpose so you have to write those down too." She was quiet a while. "Is the knee in the Log, or in the grey book?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:"The grey book. It's about him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:He did not take the grey book out. He had not taken it out for Dravin and he did not take it out now. It was in his pocket with the page on Wendel inside it. There was one line on that page that would have been worth more to her tonight than everything else on it together, and she had told him on the post four mornings ago that she had watched the man herself and did not want it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:279:"What's it for?" He tipped his head back down the hill toward the Ironyard, and seemed to mean all of it at once: the bench, the grey book, the two fingers on the wrist, the column ruled with a straightedge. "The ones I've met who fight like you, it's for something plain. Coin. A number in a book. A father. I've watched you longer than three weeks now. It isn't any of those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — Load First
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:11:He did not want the last fortnight. A fortnight could be kept honestly by anybody who knew there was a stranger at the end of the table, and he had not walked all this way to find out whether the woman could be honest for a fortnight. So he had gone to the front of the current volume and come forward a page at a time, slowly, the way his grandmother's builder had once taught him to walk a new floor: weight on each board before you trust the next, and the load first, and the ornament never.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:63:She was in the arch of the alcove with her staff on her shoulder and a cloth round her wrist that she no longer bothered to explain. Something had changed in her these last weeks. The way she stood had not; the way she came at him had. She still lost. She had lost twice since the clock, once to an Iron-equivalent Shield who sat her down in the fourth, and once to a Wind from the north gate who was better than anybody had known. But the losses had changed shape. They went longer and came out closer, and twice now she had come home from one with a thing she had learned and the bruise that bought it, and been happy about both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:67:"From the very front. Vell said." She took the cloth off her wrist and put it back on again, tighter. "Are you going to talk to him?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:75:They worked through the morning. She came in, and in, and in, as she did now, and he met her with his feet, and twice with the step. Each time the hip told him about it from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee, the old line, the price he knew. Between rounds he sat on the bench and wrote in the grey book. He wrote in the new order now. *What it's made of* came first, then the hooks, then last and smallest the old list of what a fighter did. He had built the clock's page that way, and Dessa's, and the river-academy man's, and five more since. Every new page went slower than the old ones had, and every new one would still be true next season.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:111:His own had come out Iron Skin, Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:113:He had stood in the station's bare room under the Arbiter with his mother's hand on his shoulder, and heard it read out in the clerk's flat voice. Iron Skin was a Path filed among the defensive ones, the kind that kept you whole. He was Copper, the first metal, with ten ranks to climb before anybody would look at him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:165:And he had found the boy the registry could not sort, on the east bench with a grey book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:171:The read was part of the Path. It had come with the hardness at fourteen, in the same breath. It was a sense of the other person's weight, and not the weight of their body only. He had never found a way to say what it was like that satisfied him. The nearest was the beams of an old house: when you sat in a room with your hand flat on the timber, you could feel who was moving on the floor above you. You felt a heavy step or a light one, a child running, a man carrying something and setting it down. Every practitioner pressed on that sense a little, in a shape their Path gave them and their rank sharpened. A Blade was a thin hard line leaning forward. A Force was a slow loading, like a cart being filled sack by sack, and then a crack. A Wind was weight that was there on one board and then, with no step between, on the next. The unkindled were boards with nobody on them. In two years and three circuits he had felt so many shapes that he had stopped expecting new ones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:5:He lay where he was and did not reach for the lamp. There was a thing he did on mornings that mattered, and he had done it since the spring without ever deciding to, which was to go over himself from the feet up before he let the day have any of him. Feet, warm under the blanket; ankles sound; knees sound, and the bruise on the outside of the right one, where Lira's staff had found it a fortnight ago, had gone the yellow of old paper and stopped minding being touched. The left hip was quiet: it had not burst for four days, and the line from the crest of the bone to the inside of the knee had faded until he had to press for it. Shoulder and ribs, Lira's again, had faded too, and the left wrist, which complained in the cold, was not complaining. His breath went in to a count of four and out to a count of six without his having to make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:The grey book lay beside the Log with a scrap of string in it at Friday's page. He opened it there, because he had promised himself he would read the plan once in daylight and then never again until it was over. *Three answers. Then the fourth, low, into the recovery.* Under that were the four *wrong if*s, and under them the line about Brom and the column, which he had staked the plan on. He read it all once, slowly, as he would have read another fighter's page on the morning of a bout. He found nothing in it he would change and nothing in it he was sure of, which was what a plan was supposed to look like on the morning it met somebody. Then he shut the grey book on the string and put it in his coat pocket, where it would stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:29:At the window, below the frost line, the roofs went down the hill toward the river in grey steps. The Ironyard's roof was the long dark one at the bottom with the smoke going up from the sweepers' fire. Tonight it would have every lamp Dace owned lit under it. He stood and looked at it the way you look at a stretch of road you are going to walk later, and thought, *I'm going to lose there tonight*. Then he waited to see what he felt about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:89:The heavyset man's wife came away from the stove at last, wiping her hands, and put a small parcel into his coat pocket on the side away from the grey book, without asking whether he wanted it, as she put things on Lira's plate. It was bread and a piece of the hard yellow cheese she kept on the top shelf where the sister could not reach it, wrapped in a cloth that smelled of onions. She did not say what it was for. She patted the pocket once, flat, and went back to her pans, and the sister looked at the pocket with deep suspicion and said nothing at all, which in that kitchen was a very great tribute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:103:She took him to the cobbler at the bottom of the tannery lane about a boot that did not need mending, and stood over the cobbler while he looked at it and agreed that it did not. She took him to the paper stall for a bottle of ink, when Cael had a bottle of ink at home with two-thirds left in it, and made him carry it. She took him the long way round to the lamp-oil seller's, by the river wall, so that they had to walk the whole length of the eel market in the cold with the gulls going over. And all the way she talked to him about nothing at all: about the dock partner's broken nose, how many times it had been broken, and whether it would be straighter or crookeder if somebody broke it again the other way; about the sister's hen; about a Stone pair who had fought to a standstill on the side floor the week before and then both refused to put a hand up first, so that Dace had to go out and stand between them. He knew what she was doing. She was keeping his hands busy and his head out of the grey book, and she was doing it as she did everything, without asking him whether he wanted it done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:107:Only once did she talk about the bout, and that was at the river wall, with the gulls quarrelling over something on the mud below and the water going by grey and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-23.md:259:"Brom. Iron Skin Path. Iron-equivalent." The pen moved. "Cael. Assessed. Copper-equivalent." It moved again. "The rule in this room is that nobody is killed on my floor. Both of you know it. Both of you say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:The sweepers had not come yet. The floor was grey and empty and smelled of last night's lamp oil and the cold coming up off the stone. Vell was already at her table, as she always was, with the book shut in front of her and a second stool set at the end of the table, the side nearest the door. Cael had never seen a second stool at Vell's table. Nobody sat at Vell's table. People stood at the end of it and read, and went away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:47:"It's a thing Vell does." Lira shut the book and held it on her knees with both hands, as she had held the wrist cloth the morning Brom first came to the alcove. "I asked her what was in the back cover. What she wrote, after the Dravin month. You know what she said?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:*BROM. IRON SKIN. IRON-EQUIVALENT.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:151:Cael wrote it in the grey book standing up, with the book against the rope post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:167:The newcomers were there, and two more from Keth's ring behind them, and among the three he knew best the girl with sacking in her hair went first. Cael sat on the bench at the back of Brom's alcove with the grey book open and his fingers on his wrist, and watched her go at Brom's forearm with her eyes open, all the way, as Keth had said. She did not flinch when her own blow came back past her ear. She watched it go, turned with it, and set her feet again, and looked at Brom with an expression of great concentration.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:187:Cael kept his face still. He did not look at the page, or at the shin, or anywhere in particular. He wrote nothing. He sat with his fingers on his wrist and let his eyes rest on the tall lad, who was rubbing his foot and grinning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:203:"You sat at the back for a month, by the water barrel. I counted you there. Then you came down and sat on the end of my bench, close enough to touch my sleeve, and you sat there two exchanges and went. Then three weeks at the rope. Not the back bench. The rope." Cael closed the grey book on his finger. "And now you sit there, a bench's length off, every day, and I've watched you not look at me. It's close, whatever you do. It doesn't reach far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:235:Cael found he had put his hand flat on the cover of the grey book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:311:He thought about the grey book, and about every page in it, and tried to think of one that had nobody else in it. He could not. Dessa was in it, and the clock, and the river-academy man, and Keth, and Ulric, and Corrin with his thick knuckles, and Hesk's mill-wheel on a page he had thought was only about Brom. Lira was on nearly every page, mostly in the margin, mostly as *L.*, as though she were a measurement and not a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:323:When she had gone up, he went to the crate desk and lit the lamp and opened the grey book to a clean page, and before he wrote anything else he uncapped the ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:He went back first, through the whole of the two weeks, and wherever he had written *L.* he wrote *Lira* above it, small, in ink. It took a long time. There were more of them than he had thought, and some of them were on pages that had nothing to do with her at all, in the margins of other people's fights. On the page about the middle column he wrote *Keth asked*, and on the coverage map he wrote *her partner*, and on the first page with Brom's name at the head of it, under *Iron Skin. Formal tier not known*, he wrote in ink, *Brom*, again, and then, because she had told him to and because it was true, a line round it with corners like a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Brom was not there when he came in at the side door before light. The Ironyard was black and cold, and the sweepers had not come, and the only lamp lit was the one Cael carried. He went across the floor to the second alcove, Brom's, and hung the lamp on its hook, and sat on the bench against the back wall where he had sat for twelve days with his fingers on his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:77:From the bench the hold had been a fist stopping dead on an arm. From his own knuckles it was the feeling of a blow going down out of his hand into a man and not coming back for the length of a beat. It went down the way a stone goes down a well, so that he could feel the whole weight of what he had thrown leave him and go somewhere far below. Then the turn came up out of the well, and it was his own weight returned to him with an angle in it, through the bones of the hand and the wrist and the elbow, and his arm swung wide on a line he had never sent it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:79:He came back to his cross and did it again. And again. He hit the forearm, the shoulder, the chest, the forearm, while the alcove went from black to grey round the lamp and the cold came up out of the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:105:By the end of the hour both his forearms were blue from the wrist to the elbow on the inside, where the returns had come up through them, and his hands had swollen enough that the knuckles had gone smooth. He sat on the bench afterward to write, and found he could not close his fingers properly round the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:189:If he kept his attention entirely in the skin of his own knuckles and nowhere else, he could feel which way the door was hung before it opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:285:"In the skin. If you keep all of yourself there and nowhere else." Cael shrugged, and his shoulder told him not to. "I don't know if it's true of anybody but him. I've only had my hand on him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-27.md:293:At home Lira was at the kitchen table with the lamp, with her own wrist in a fresh cloth and her bad arm gone from plum to green. She looked at his forearms when he took his coat off, both of them, the insides blue from wrist to elbow, and counted under her breath without seeming to know she was doing it. Then she got up and fetched the bucket from the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:It was a week to the day since the main floor, a Tuesday again, grey and very cold, with a skin of ice on the water barrel by the side door that the night man had broken with his heel. They had the alcove to themselves for two hours before the sweepers. Brom had hung the lamp, and taken his coat off, and stood on his cross and said nothing at all, not even *what are you testing*, because Cael had told him on the walk down from the corner. *All of it. What you are when you're not being kind.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:13:But he learned more in that hour than in the two mornings before it together. Brom at full did not only send him further; he sent him truer. The careful answers had been rounded off at the edges, softened in the turn, and now the edges were back, every one of them. He could feel the lean in the first touch as clearly as a word spoken aloud, and he found that if he kept the whole of himself in the skin of his knuckles, he knew before the turn came which wall he was going to hit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:15:He did not stop to write anything; his hands would not have let him. After a while it stopped being a test of anything. It was only a thing his body did, over and over, in the grey light, like a man splitting wood: set, swing, the shock up the arms, set again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:27:It did not settle in his hip, where the Wind lived. It did not settle low under his ribs, where the giving face sat waiting. It went outward, and spread over him from somewhere in the middle and lay down on the outside of him, thin, all over at once, the way the first frost lies on a field: on his arms and his back and his face and the palms of his hands, a fine new layer of something laid on his skin that he had not put there. And through it, for one long breath, he could feel the alcove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:31:Then it went, though the layer did not leave him; he could feel it was still there, lying on his skin. But it folded away, quiet, like a sheet folded and put in a drawer, and the door between him and the morning swung open again, and he was a boy standing in an alcove with one foot not yet down, in grey light, on a cross of chalk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Note: surface-awareness component. Pressure read, limited range.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:60:*Surface-awareness. Pressure read, limited range.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:64:*Surface* he understood, because he could feel it: the new thin layer lying on him, on the outside, where the Wind had never been and the giving face had never been. *Awareness* he thought he understood, because of the breath when he had felt the wall at his back and the lamp's warmth and the great blurred weight in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:86:"Part of it," said Cael. "The edge. The read, it says, and not much of that. Limited." He looked at his own hands, turned up on his knees, blue at the wrists. "Not the hardness. I don't think I'll get that by standing here. I don't think it works like asking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:90:"Just now. Coming back to the cross. I'd stopped thinking about anything." He touched his own forearm, the outside of it, where the new thing lay. "It went over me. On the skin, all over. And for a breath I could feel the room. The wall. You." He looked up. "You're big."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:104:They sat on the floor of the alcove facing each other in the grey light, the boy with his legs crossed and the big man on his heels, and neither of them said anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-28.md:134:He read the Pressure more slowly still: the taking face and the giving face, and the wrist, and the ceiling, *egg, fist, hole*. He read *It isn't the thing that fails past three. It's me*. And then he came to the bottom of the entry, and Cael watched his eyes go along a line, and stop, and go back to the start of it, and he knew which line it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:He lay on his back in the dark with the blanket up to his chin and the frost growing on the window, and turned his attention outward, onto his own skin, where the new thing lay. He could feel that it was there. That was the strange part: he could feel it, not as weight exactly, only as the knowledge that something is on you, like a coat worn all day. It lay over his arms and his back and his face, very thin, and it did not move, and it did not answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:13:It had been a whole season before he could ask it for anything and be sure of an answer. The giving face had been the same, in its way: two misfires up the wrist before it would ride a strike on the beat. Twice already he had started from nothing like this. He had only forgotten what nothing felt like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:29:Three paces away a man stood with the read open as far as it went, and Cael, who had a piece of that same read lying on every inch of his skin, could not feel him any more than he could feel the moon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:They stopped when the sweepers had finished and the noon light was coming grey through the high windows. Brom stood up and gathered his coat off the nail, and Cael stayed where he was on the floor, because his knees and his head between them had decided he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:159:He thought that he had spent a year learning to see everything. He had sat on the east bench with his eyes doing the work of a whole body, and been proud of what they found, and paid for it in aches and a blind spot ten degrees wide. And now there was a thing lying on his skin that did not want him to look at anything at all. It wanted him to be the floor, and let the weight come down on him, and say what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:169:*What it is: the edge of B.'s read. A thin thing on the skin, all over, outside. It doesn't go out. Things come in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:185:*Not the same thing. This came with a hush and a notice; the third came with neither. This lies on my skin and I can feel it lying there; the third left nothing on me to feel, before or after. This takes something in, for a few breaths, a man as big as a door; in the third I took nothing in. I was looking at his shoulder. This costs a band round the head; the third cost nothing I can find. This opens only when I'm still and stop aiming; the third came while I was moving, in the middle of an exchange. This is a piece of his read. The third was his read finding nobody there. One I have and can't use yet. The other I never had, and can't find. It stays where it fell.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-29.md:195:He let it drift open without meaning to. He was only sitting very still, tired, with nothing to aim at, and the thing on his skin found the stillness on its own and opened, a crack. And there through the wall, two paces off, faint, there was somebody: a light quick weight, never still for long, like a bird on a sill that keeps shifting its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:He took down everything in the press that so much as mentioned Iron Skin, which was three books. There was a big brown register of the Paths of the realm, a hundred years old and re-bound twice, and a smaller book on the defensive Paths in particular, and a guild almanac from a city up the coast with a chapter on the families who trained them. He carried all three to the table nearest the stove and sat with them through the whole of the cold afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:21:The register gave Iron Skin a line among the defensive Paths, between a Path for turning blows and another for standing, and said it hardened the body against force. It had been recorded at every metal from Copper to Gold, and at none above. The book on defensive Paths gave it a little more, and all of it was the same thing said at greater length. The almanac gave it four pages.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He read the four pages twice, because he did not at first believe them. They were about who owned it. Here were the three guild houses that taught it, and when each had been founded and by whom. Here was which of them had quarrelled with which, and which families had sent their children to which house for four generations, and what those children had become. One page was nothing but a list of marriages. And in the middle of the second page, between the founding of one house and the founding of the next, there was a single sentence on what the Path did. *Its practitioners raise a hardness of the surface of the body at need, the art of which is the property of the houses named above.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:25:He copied the sentence out exactly into the grey book. Under it he wrote: *Four pages on who owns it. One sentence on what it does. The registry's the same, only shorter.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:39:Corrin found him rather than the other way round. He was a big grey Stone fighter with a face like a split log, who had been fighting in the Ironyard since before Dace kept the slate. He no longer fought much, but he sat on the end of the front bench most nights with his hands on his stick and told whoever sat next to him what was wrong with the bout in front of them. He stopped Cael at the water barrel on the second evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:41:"You're asking round about Iron Skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:45:"Because of the big one." Corrin did not wait for an answer. "I fought one once. Years back, in a port up the coast, before your mother had you. A woman, not big. Iron Skin, Iron tier, and she looked at me like I was a horse she was thinking of buying." He worked his right hand open and shut on the head of his stick. The knuckles were thick, and two of them did not close all the way. "I'll tell you what it's like and you can make of it what you want. It's not her hitting you. She hardly hit me at all. It's your own hit. You put your best into her, everything you've got, and the hit comes back up your arm the wrong way, on a line you didn't send it on. Your wrist knows before your eyes do. Like punching water that decided, halfway, to be a wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:65:The second man Cael asked was a Blade from Orvet's gym who had lost to an Iron Skin in the second city, and he wanted mostly to talk about the injustice of it, and about a keeper who had scored it wrong, and about his own left knee. Cael listened politely for as long as he could and wrote down one word, *knee*, which turned out to be about the man and not the Path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He paid them to hit him. That was the arrangement. A copper a round, three rounds each, and they could hit him anywhere they liked as hard as they liked with anything they had. They queued up for it, and when they had had their three rounds they went back up the floor rubbing their wrists and looking pleased. Cael heard the girl with sacking in her hair say to the tall lad that it was the best copper she ever earned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:79:Cael sat on the bench against the back wall of the alcove with the grey book open on his knee and his eyes doing what his eyes did now, and by the end of the hour he had a problem.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:123:But he had no clock. Nobody in the district had a clock that told less than a quarter-hour, and that only on the chapel by the north gate, which was usually wrong. He timed everything in a bout by his own breath. But his breath went short and long when he was interested, and he was very interested, and a measure that moved with his interest was no measure. He sat a while with his fingers on the inside of his own wrist, feeling the steady beat there, slow and even in the quiet room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:127:"You're taking your own pulse," said Lira, in the morning, at the alcove, watching him sit on the bench with two fingers on his wrist and his eyes on nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:133:"I've never listened to it." He let go of his wrist. "I need to know what one beat is. Exactly. So I can say a thing took one and a half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:Lira looked at him for a moment, and then put two fingers on her own wrist and stood very still with her eyes half shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:141:"I'm not going to be able to stop doing this now," said Lira, with her fingers still on her wrist. "I'm going to be doing this all day. Thank you so much."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:183:From the benches, a newcomer's fist on Brom's forearm looked like one thing: the knock, and the newcomer flung away. From close, with his fingers on his wrist, it was two things with a stillness between them. The knock came first. Then the fist stayed where it had landed, stopped dead against the arm, and did not bounce, while Brom's knees gave a finger's width and the weight of the blow went down through him into his heels, the way Hesk's water went into the gears before the wheel turned. Then the arm turned, and the newcomer went off sideways with his own fist flung wide. That stillness, from the knock to the throw, was what Cael counted, and he called it the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:In all the hours Cael had watched him, Brom had never once meant a blow; he waited on his heels for other people to mean them, and so there would be nothing to step away from. Cael could burst to the front-left and the left and the back-left all night, paying half a breath and a line of hip every time, while Brom paid nothing. And Cael would end up standing in the middle of the main floor with grey at the edges of his eyes and a hip on fire, having made a very big man turn round on the spot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:29:And the giving face, the one he used. Ridden into a strike on the beat, it put a weight into his fist that his arm alone could never have found, and it left the hollow under his ribs after. Off the beat it went back up his own arm, which was why his left wrist ached in the cold. Three to a sequence, about, and he had never tried a fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:39:*Everything I've built this year, both halves of it: against him one's a spare coat, and the other's a knife with the handle pointed at me. What's left: my feet. My hands. The middle column. Whatever I can find in two weeks of watching a man who wants to be watched. If I win this, the grey book wins it. The Log sits on the bench.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:That was the page. He had meant to watch him a second time and had sat in Brom's alcove instead, with his fingers on his wrist, and so the page was what he took to the rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:183:"In the big one's alcove." She did not look up. "Half the floor's seen you sitting there with your fingers on your wrist like a doctor. They think you're ill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:63:For months there had been a line in the Pressure entry with nothing after it but a question. *Upper limit of the giving face under sustained use: not known.* He had never wanted to find it by going past it. He still had the second sprain in his left wrist in cold weather, and that had been an off-beat misfire, nothing like the top of anything; he had no wish at all to learn what the top felt like from the far side. So he did what he had done in the winter with Lira's stick. He did not go looking for the edge. He walked toward it a step at a time, slowly, and meant to stop as soon as he could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:But he had not expected the fingers on the wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:The boy sat on the bench against the back wall of Brom's alcove with his book open on his knee and two fingers of his left hand laid on the inside of his right wrist, and his eyes, while the newcomers came at Brom, went somewhere Brom could not follow. They went still. And when the boy with the broken tooth swung and Brom answered him, the boy on the bench moved his lips very slightly, counting, and wrote something in a column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:97:Nobody had ever timed that. His instructors at home had watched the hardening and the hits it turned, and his opponents had watched what came back up their arms. In two years and three circuits, nobody had ever sat down on a bench and asked how long it took him to make up his mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:111:The bench where the boy sat was not much more than the length of a bench away from where Brom stood, and so, without meaning to, every afternoon, Brom read him. He could not help it any more than he could help hearing a voice in the next room. And the boy sitting still with his fingers on his wrist read as he had read on the east bench: a weight that was not where a weight ought to be, not empty, quiet. Once, on the fifth day, when one of the newcomers fell wrong and the boy's whole body leaned toward catching her before he caught himself, the weight moved in him as it moved in his fights: the short Wind-like flicker, there and gone, from a boy who had not even stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:Brom was not hard all over. That was the first thing the drawing told him, and he would not have believed it if he had not spent seven sessions watching where the newcomers' hits went and what came back from each, because the hardening went only where Brom trusted it to find the hit cleanly: his lead forearm, the left, which he held loose across his front, took the most and turned the most back. So did the outer edge of his lead foot, when he set it to meet something; once, when the tall lad aimed a kick at it, he had turned the edge of the foot into the kick and sent the lad's whole leg back past him as if the stone had shrugged. Across the chest and the shoulders it was there, but less, and the answers came slower and sent the newcomers less far. And between that foot and the knee, on the shin and the ankle and the back of the calf, Cael had almost nothing written at all. Below the knee, the man was very nearly only a man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:201:It might be that the hardening could not reach that far easily, from wherever in the man it came from; or it might be that in two years nobody had ever made him pay for leaving it bare, since newcomers did not know to kick low and most fighters in the Ironyard aimed at the middle of a man, because that was where a man was. Cael wrote both possibilities down beside the gap below the knee, and underlined the gap, and did not let himself decide which was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:233:Cael sat on the bench with his fingers on his wrist and the middle column ruled, and watched Brom work properly for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:*Not the hold; the hold got shorter all hour. Not the breath before; it was there before every one of the three. It's the recovery, and it's the only one of the three that gets longer the more he does. Against the post half a beat; live, after three fast, two. So it isn't a moment; it's an account. Three hard answers draw it down, and the fourth comes slow because there's less in it to fill him. What does the hardening cost him? He pays for it somehow; everybody pays for everything. If three of them slow the fourth, it's drawing on something that runs out. What's he like with the account at nothing? And how long does it take him to fill it?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:19:Lira got the boots off and rolled his trousers to the thigh and looked at his knees in the candlelight without saying anything. They had begun to come up, both of them, round and tight and hot, the skin over them shiny, and the right was worse than the left. She laid her palm flat on the right one, very lightly, and he made a sound he had not meant to make.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:He had a column for what every one of them cost him to use. He had kept it since the spring: the half-breath, the lock, the hip, the hollow, the wrist. He had priced every one of them down to the copper, and he had never in his life thought to price the other thing, the carrying, the cost of having a thing in you and keeping it shut while it leaned. And it had cost him. It had cost him twice tonight, in ground, on the main floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:189:He had come home from the station Iron Skin and Copper with his mother's hand on his shoulder. For three weeks after, nobody in the house said one cruel word. He was very particular about that; he said it twice. Nobody shouted, and nobody sent him to eat in the kitchen. Only the long table went quiet. It went quiet in the way of a room where somebody has just stopped talking about you, every evening, when he came in to supper. His grandmother asked about his day in a voice he had heard her use once before, to a neighbour whose barn had burned. His sister wrote from the coast and did not mention it. His mother kept touching his shoulder in passing. And in the mornings the yard man, who had stood over him with a stick since he was twelve, sat him down with his back against the estate wall and told him to stay there and not move until he was called. He did that every morning for a month, while the house decided what to do with him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:"Then build toward the name and operate without it," said Brom. "You walk the one while you're looking for the other. Nobody's made you choose." He turned the bread over. "I did that. With Iron Skin. The houses had their word for it, and their four pages. I had what it did on the post at night. I went on doing what it did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:275:Cael did. He was already seeing it: Iron Skin from arm's length instead of a bench's, the breath and the hold and the recovery not on his wrist but through his own knuckles, the man not as a column but as a thing he could feel. It would cost more, much more, than sitting on a bench. Every number he took would be bought with a bruise. It would also be the first true map he had ever had of anything that hit back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-26.md:293:"You'll have it." The third finger. "If one of us is hurt past the point of learning anything from it, that's the end of the morning. Not the end of the round. Not when we've nearly got it. Then." Brom put his hand back on the bread. "In the river city I trained with a man who finished every round he ever started. He finished one on a cracked wrist, to prove he could. He never finished anything after that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:65:In the end he took the boy's wrist in a grip that the boy's legs could not have broken even fresh, and turned him on it, slowly. He set him down on the stone with a hand on his shoulder, and kept the turn off the knees, because he knew to the inch what he had already asked of those knees in the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-25.md:131:Lira was there; he had not seen her come under the rope. She knelt by his legs and put her good hand on him, flat, quick and certain, as she checked her own wrist after a bout: his ankles, his shins, his knees, pressing, watching his face while she pressed. She did his hips, and his ribs, and then she took his chin and turned his head to look at his eyes in the lamplight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-24.md:147:And he had not breathed like that against the newcomers, because they were never fast enough to need it. Against them the read and the fist had come together, and he had breathed on whichever came; Cael had sat on the bench with his fingers on his wrist and timed the version of the man who was fighting twelve-year-olds. Somebody, somewhere, in a boat shed or a city by the coast, had tried this very thing before Cael was ever born into it. Some fast Wind with good legs had tried to arrive before the hardness, and had made Brom learn to harden on the read instead of the knock, and Brom had kept the lesson.

