   126	
   127	It was a plain room, like all the rooms on that corridor, with a bed and a press and a table under the window, and it was tidier than Cael's because Brom was. The two boxes were under the bed, roped, where they had been since the week of the gate, and Cael had stopped seeing them. The plaque, dark wood and a brass plate with the bar and ring, lay on the shelf on its face.
   128	
   129	Brom was standing at the table with his coat off and laid over the chair, and he was emptying its left inside pocket.
   130	
   131	He did it slowly, a thing at a time, as he did everything. The summons, soft at the folds. The first docket. The district's confirmation, with *anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand. The schedule of evidence. He unfolded each of them and flattened it under his palm and laid it on the one before, until there was a small squared stack at the table's left-hand corner, and then he set the whetstone in its sock on top to hold them.
   132	
   133	Then he took out the envelope.
   134	
   135	Cael had not seen it out of that pocket since the day it came. It was square, of a heavy cream paper, a little rubbed at the corners now from eighteen weeks of being carried against a man's ribs. The seal on its flap was a dark red wax with a device pressed into it that he had never been close enough to read. The seal had not been broken; there was not so much as a crack across it.
   136	
   137	Brom held it for a moment in both hands, and then he laid it on the table, by itself, a hand's breadth from the stack and a hand's breadth from the edge, with the seal uppermost. He put one thick finger on each of its near corners and turned it a very little, until its side lay true with the table's. He looked at it, and moved it the width of a nail, and looked again.
   138	
   139	He took his hands away.
   140	
   141	"Four weeks," he said. "To the day. I worked it out on the box, coming down, last Fifth-day, after the hood said it." He did not look round. "I thought I'd like to stop carrying it."
   142	
   143	Cael stood in the doorway and did not say anything.
   144	
   145	He had never asked. None of them had. It had been agreed in the recess before the year opened, in about as many words as it takes to pass the salt, that what came to Brom from Velmere was Brom's, and that nobody would ask what it offered or what he meant to answer. He had seen Brom's face, once, on the evening the first of this year's letters was opened, and had gone on not asking. He had watched that hand go to that pocket a dozen times a day through a road and a cache and a recess. He did not know one word of what was in either envelope.
   146	
   147	"I know you've none of you asked," said Brom. He might have heard him think it. "All year. I noticed. I heard you agree it, in the recess, Lira and Karis and you. I was coming back along the walk with the bread and I stopped to let you finish." He turned round at last and sat down on the edge of the bed, heavily, with his forearms on his knees. "It's the kindest thing anybody's done for me on this hill. I'd like four more weeks of it, if that's all right."
   148	
   149	"It's all right."
   150	
   151	"I thought it would be." He looked across the room at the table. "It can sit there. Where I can see it. In my coat it was getting so I couldn't put my arm down properly, and I've a frame to stand in." He rubbed his face with both hands. "I'm not opening it. I want that said to somebody. I'm not opening it before the panel and I'm not opening it before the ruling. After that I'll know what I'm answering."
   152	
   153	He did not say what he would be answering, but got up, and went to the press, and came back with a coil of brown strap leather and the awl.
   154	
   155	"Give me the satchel," he said. "Third hole. He told you, didn't he? I'd have said the fourth."
   156	
   157	Cael gave him the satchel, and sat on the end of the bed and watched Brom measure the old strap against the new with his thumb, and cut it, and set the buckle, Hesk's buckle, with the bevel, onto the fresh leather with two rivets and the flat of the whetstone.
   158	
   159	On the table under the window the envelope lay square to the edge, with its red seal up, in the last of the afternoon's light.
   160	
   161	Neither of them looked at it again, and both of them knew exactly where it was.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	The year's last month opened on a First-day, and Bracken turned his card.
   166	
   167	He kept it in his waistcoat, the registry's own pocket calendar, a stiff card printed on both faces. Cael was at the counter when he took it out, with the second glass on his nose, and read its near face down to the foot, and turned it over with a small dry snap. He laid it on the counter under the lamp and looked at its other face for a moment, as a ship's master might look at a chart of the last stretch into port.
   168	
   169	"There," said Bracken.
   170	
   171	He took up the chalk and went to the hall's own board, the long slate by the press where the house's sittings were written. He rubbed out the eleventh with the heel of his hand and wrote three lines in its place, square and plain.
   172	
   173	*Closing sitting: the second Fifth-day.*
   174	
   175	*Closing sitting: the third Fifth-day.*
   176	
   177	*Ruling: the last Fifth-day. The house will attend.*
   178	
   179	And under those, after a moment, smaller, because it was not the house's business and he was a man who knew the difference:
   180	
   181	*District panel (Copper to Iron): the same day, the second bell.*
   229	---
   230	
   231	That night he sat a long time at the sill before he wrote.
   232	
   233	The window was open a hand's breadth, and the air that came in was milder than it had been, and smelled of wet stone. Below him the court was dark, and the fountain went up and came down unheard under the noise of four hundred people going to bed. Across the roofs one lamp burned in the window at the top of the library, turned up as high as every night, so that the valley would see what it always saw.
   234	
   235	At the foot of the bluff road, small and yellow, the coats had their two lanterns lit.
   236	
   237	He thought about them for a while, the four men down there, senior, patient, changed on the bell. They had stood at that road since before the year opened, and they had been waiting all that time for one thing, a change in a column. A status. The riders had come, and the road, and after that nothing; and they had gone on standing there through a recess and eleven sittings with their tin cup, because the thing they were waiting for was being brought to them, a Fifth-day at a time, by the people they were watching.
   238	
   239	He had helped to bring it. He had sat up straight in a coach eleven times.
   240	
   241	He did not think that had been wrong, though he turned it over once more, to be sure, as Karis turned a leaf she already knew. If he had gone east in the week of the case, the word would be on four files now and a line on Withrow's charter, and thirty-four chancellors would have had their answer from a hood instead of from her pen. He had stayed, and gone to every sitting, and kept his face still behind a first-year with a new badge. He had won the nine days and Gault's notes and the ninety-one, and watched each of them go into the minute and weigh nothing.
   242	
   243	It had cost what it cost. Karis was writing thirty slips for a room she might not be let speak in. Lira had landed a thousand times on a foot, for a sitting she had read the date of and called by its right name. Brom had a letter on his table that he could not open and a frame he meant to stand in honestly. Seln had a bundle as thick as his hand. Withrow had ink to the knuckle.
   244	
   245	And he had a year of bouts in which he had known the answer half a beat early and waited.
   246	
   247	None of them had said they were leaving. He did not think any of them had decided it, in so many words, even alone. It was only that there were now three boxes on a shelf, and two under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat, and a new strap on a satchel, and five beans in a gardener's jar.
   248	
   249	He opened Hesk's book and wrote.
   250	
   251	*First-day. The year's last month.*
   252	
   253	*Eight, unchanged. The new one idle; it wants thresholds and takes names instead. The quiet one sealed, on his terms, paid for every morning. Under the eight, the still place, uncounted.*
   254	
   255	*The seat: two closing sittings and a ruling, the last on the month's last Fifth-day. Leave for Karis to be heard is "to be considered at the close." Brom's panel sits the same day, at the second bell, three streets off.*
   256	
   257	*On Brom's table: one envelope, sealed, square to the edge. Four weeks now, less a few days.*
   258	
   259	*In the records hall*
   260	
   261	He stopped, and looked at the three words for some time.
   262	
   263	Then he drew one straight line through them, so that they could still be read, and wrote beside them in the margin, small.
    25	"Are these your notes upon the enrollee for the current academic year?"
    26	
    27	"They are."
    28	
    29	"Made by you, at the time?"
    30	
    31	"Each before I left the room in which the thing was observed. Where I could not write at once, I have said so in the margin, and by how long."
    32	
    33	"Entire?"
    34	
    35	"Entire." Gault's hand was still on the board. "I was asked for the notes upon the enrollee. I have taken that to mean every note in which he appears, and not only the notes of his bouts. There are seventeen panel notes, one to a supervised session, with the three cards and the figure. There are also five notes that are not of any panel." He lifted the hand. "I mention it so that the seat is not surprised by the leaf for the year's seventy-sixth day."
    36	
    37	Something moved under the hood. It was the smallest alteration, a stillness becoming a different stillness, and Cael, who had watched that grey shape for eight sittings, felt it in the back of his neck.
    38	
    39	The seventy-sixth day. Four riders at the gate, and an order read aloud under the arch.
    40	
    41	"The clerk will read the contents leaf," said the hood.
    42	
    43	The clerk read it: seventeen sessions by date, each with its floor and its figure, and then the five. A note on the enrollee's excusal for injury, with the healer's line; two notes on the visitors' gallery; a note on the production of the wing's records to this seat. And the fifth, which the clerk read out in the same voice as the others, because it was written in the same hand.
    44	
    45	"*Seventy-sixth day. An order was presented at the house's gate, by four riders, in the name of an office styled facilities, for the enrollee's attendance upon an assessment. No assessment of this enrollee has been notified to the evaluator of record, by any office, at any time. I have written that down.*"
    46	
    47	The long room was very quiet.
    48	
    49	Jent had risen, and stood with his hands at his sides and his spectacles hanging, and for the first time in eight sittings Cael thought he saw the man choose his words.
    50	
    51	"The petitioner has no knowledge of the matter in that note," said Jent. "I say so for the minute, and I say it carefully. It is no part of the petition. No instrument of the kind described was sought by the petitioner or issued at his instance." He paused. "As to the rest of the volume: the petitioner accepts it, as he said he would. He relies on none of it."
    52	
    53	"Received," said the hood. "Entire. And entered."
    54	
    55	That was all. The clerk wrote, and at the records table the junior chair drew the volume toward him and opened his receipt book. In the middle chair below the platform, Ilsev had turned a leaf in her file and was writing something small at its foot, and she did not look at the house's table, and she did not look at the hood.
    56	
    57	Cael sat with his hands flat on the wood.
    58	
    59	A magister had just put into a registry seat's own minute, under its own order, in a clerk's level voice, that somebody had tried to take the respondent off his hill by a paper nobody would own. It had been received, and it would lie in the registry's presses beside the table of renewals for as long as there was a registry.
   189	She did not get up at once.
   190	
   191	The lantern hissed, and up in the dark of the gallery something small moved along the rail and was still. She sat with the book on her knee and her bare right foot flat on the cold boards, on purpose, and let herself think about the sheet.
   192	
   193	Four losses. Rooke would send her program sheet down to the district seat with the meet record, when the time came, and a clerk at a counter would lay the two side by side. On one, a figure of twenty-seven against a Silver of eleven years' standing. On the other, *Not fought. Not fought. Not fought.* and a red flag from a girl three ranks up who was her friend. The clerk would not know about a ditch. There was no column on the sheet for ditches.
   194	
   195	She found that she minded. That surprised her a little. She had thought, after the reading room, after the forty pairs and the father's letter that she had read aloud in her own voice, that she would never again be able to care what a clerk at a counter laid beside what. She knew what the counter was. She knew what sat behind every certificate in the wing's drawer, her own among them, three lines in a hand she had never seen.
   196	
   197	She had wondered about that, once. Only once, on the second night, on the stair. What had the keeper read on her, before anybody wrote it down? She remembered a brass plate, and cold fingers turning her by the shoulder, and being told to stand still. She did not remember anybody saying a word she could use.
   198	
   199	She had put it away on the same stair. There was no finding out. And she had spent four years being told what she was by people who had never watched her fight, and she was not going to start telling herself the same kind of story now, in the other direction, with no more to go on than they had.
   200	
   201	Wind, first rank. That was what the paper said. What she was, she was writing down herself, in pencil, in a carter's book.
   202	
   203	And still she wanted the ladder. She could say that, here, where nobody would write it. Rooke had rebuilt a hall for her. He had brought a man from the river to stand on crosses, and bought a red book, and put her name on a slate in his own hand, and nobody had ever done that for her in her life. At Fenmark they had told her that her form was correct and her room was wrong, and shown her the yard out the back. For a year after that she had stood at guild doors. This was the first door anybody had built to her measure and held open.

