books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-300-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-301-The stakes, paid in order. **Brom's chapter-within-the-chapter:** the advancement review's finding arrives mid-council, hand-delivered — advancement to Iron-tier, found and recommended, his family's whole ledger settled in one paragraph — with the guidance note appended in flawless registry prose: *advancement suitability weighs continued association with enrollees under active non-compliance proceedings.* Everything he ever wanted, priced at Cael. The chapter gives him the series' full weight-without-speeches treatment: he reads it once, sets it on the table face-up so everyone can price it too, and asks Karis one question — "How many boxes is your research?" Karis: "Three." Brom, standing: "Then I'm carrying three boxes." The review is never answered. **Lira:** the Silver program named for what it is — the first ladder ever built toward her — and declined in one sentence that completes her Book 5 arc: "I said I wanted to become something they haven't seen yet. Turns out it doesn't live here." **Karis:** the forfeiture named precisely — ranking, standing, budget, the first institution that ever resourced her properly — and her answer is the researcher's: she began packing the compiled materials two days ago; the decision, for her, was a finding, and she'd already entered it. **Seln:** asked nothing, volunteers one sentence: "I resigned fifteen years ago. I just never filed it." **Ephram** — invited to the council's end, told nothing of the cache, offered the truth of the departure — asks for one thing before they go (Chapter 20's premise, set here). The vote is unanimous and the chapter refuses to dramatize it; the drama was the pricing.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md-302-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:303:*Close on:* Withrow, informed alone, in her office, by Cael — the respondent chancellor learning her academy's defense ends by resignation. Her registry-trained arithmetic runs it in front of him: the resignation protects Halcenvane, ends the proceedings as moot, and forfeits nothing that wasn't already forfeit. Then the other column, unperformed: "I spent my tenure building an institution worth measuring. You were the best case it ever held." A pause, and the line the book banks for later arcs: "The record will say you left. I'll spend the rest of my tenure making the record say why." The log: "Five weeks became five days. We leave before the date. All of us. Nobody gets corrected."
