editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:7:The budget line was small. Cael had seen the figure in the house's accounts, on the leaf the council had sat twice to head: a sum for copying and couriers and archive fees, the kind of money a senior instructor spent in a year without noticing where it had gone. Karis had not touched it in five weeks. She had built an eleven-pound answer to the Compact without spending a mark of it, because the answer was the house's, and the house paid for the house's paper. This was the first thing she had wanted for herself.
